// Rep Max questions: Chemistry (AQA 7405). One question per line, no commas between lines.
// Fields: id (unique), s (subject), t (topic id from data/spec.js), lv (1 warm-up, 2 working set, 3 one-rep max),
// q (question; maths between $ signs, backslashes doubled), code (optional), o (four options), a (index 0-3 of the right one), x (explanation).
// A line that isn't valid is skipped and listed at the bottom of the home screen. Lines starting // are ignored.
{"id":"ch-001","s":"chem","t":"3.1.1","lv":1,"q":"What is the electron configuration of a Ca²⁺ ion?","o":["1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶","1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s²","1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s¹","1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d²"],"a":0,"x":"Calcium (Z = 20) is 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s². Forming Ca²⁺ removes both 4s electrons, leaving the same configuration as argon.","w":["","This is the neutral Ca atom; the 2+ charge means both 4s electrons are lost.","Only one 4s electron removed, which gives Ca⁺, not Ca²⁺.","Ca has no 3d electrons; forming Ca²⁺ simply removes the two 4s electrons."]}
{"id":"ch-002","s":"chem","t":"3.1.1","lv":1,"q":"In a time of flight mass spectrometer, ²⁰Ne⁺ and ²²Ne⁺ ions are accelerated to the same kinetic energy. Which statement is correct?","o":["²²Ne⁺ travels faster, so it reaches the detector first","Both reach the detector together because they have the same charge","²⁰Ne⁺ travels faster, so it reaches the detector first","²⁰Ne⁺ travels slower because lighter ions are deflected more"],"a":2,"x":"KE = ½mv², so for the same KE the lighter ion has the greater speed. It crosses the flight tube in less time and arrives first.","w":["Reversed: at equal KE the heavier ion is slower, so ²²Ne⁺ arrives later.","Same charge doesn't mean same speed; speed depends on mass at equal KE.","","TOF has no deflection; at equal KE the lighter ²⁰Ne⁺ is faster, not slower."]}
{"id":"ch-003","s":"chem","t":"3.1.1","lv":2,"q":"Why is the first ionisation energy of aluminium lower than that of magnesium?","o":["Al has a smaller nuclear charge than Mg","Al's outer electron is removed from a 3p orbital containing a pair of electrons","Al's outer electron is in a 3p sub-shell, higher in energy and shielded by 3s","Al atoms are smaller than Mg atoms, so the outer electron is closer"],"a":2,"x":"Al's outer electron is in 3p, which is higher in energy than 3s and partly shielded by the 3s electrons. Al has more protons than Mg, not fewer.","w":["Al has 13 protons, Mg has 12, so Al's nuclear charge is larger, not smaller.","Al has only one 3p electron, so there is no pair; pairing explains P vs S.","","Al is smaller, but a closer electron would raise the IE, not lower it."]}
{"id":"ch-004","s":"chem","t":"3.1.1","lv":2,"q":"Boron has two isotopes: ¹⁰B (19.9%) and ¹¹B (80.1%). What is the relative atomic mass of boron to 1 decimal place?","o":["10.5","10.8","10.2","11.0"],"a":1,"x":"(10 × 19.9 + 11 × 80.1) ÷ 100 = 10.801, so 10.8. The simple mean (10.5) ignores abundances, and 10.2 swaps the abundances.","w":["Simple mean of 10 and 11; it ignores the isotope abundances.","","Swapped the abundances, so the 19.9% was applied to ¹¹B.","Rounded to the heavier mass number; weight each mass by its abundance."]}
{"id":"ch-005","s":"chem","t":"3.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A sample of magnesium gives mass spectrum peaks at m/z 24, 25 and 26 with relative heights 79, 10 and 11. What is the relative atomic mass to 1 decimal place?","o":["25.0","24.0","24.8","24.3"],"a":3,"x":"(24 × 79 + 25 × 10 + 26 × 11) ÷ (79 + 10 + 11) = 2432 ÷ 100 = 24.32, so 24.3. Weight each mass by its peak height, then divide by the total height.","w":["Simple mean of 24, 25 and 26; peak heights were ignored.","Just the most abundant isotope; the other peaks must be included.","Too high: m/z 24 makes up 79% of the total, so Ar must sit close to 24.",""]}
{"id":"ch-006","s":"chem","t":"3.1.2","lv":1,"q":"What amount, in mol, is present in 5.30 g of sodium carbonate, Na₂CO₃? (Na 23.0, C 12.0, O 16.0)","o":["0.0189","0.0500","0.500","0.106"],"a":1,"x":"Mr of Na₂CO₃ = 2(23.0) + 12.0 + 3(16.0) = 106.0. Moles = mass ÷ Mr = 5.30 ÷ 106.0 = 0.0500 mol.","w":["Wrong Mr used; Mr of Na₂CO₃ is 106.0, so moles = 5.30 ÷ 106.0.","","Decimal slip: this is ten times too big.","This is 5.30 ÷ 50; divide the mass by Mr, which is 106.0."]}
{"id":"ch-007","s":"chem","t":"3.1.2","lv":2,"q":"Ethanol is made by CH₃CH₂Cl + NaOH → CH₃CH₂OH + NaCl. What is the atom economy for ethanol? (H 1.0, C 12.0, O 16.0, Na 23.0, Cl 35.5)","o":["71.3%","56.0%","44.0%","88.0%"],"a":2,"x":"Atom economy = Mr of desired product ÷ total Mr of reactants × 100 = 46.0 ÷ (64.5 + 40.0) × 100 = 44.0%. Both reactants count in the total.","w":["Divided by Mr of CH₃CH₂Cl only; NaOH must be in the total too.","This is 100 − 44.0, the percentage waste, not the atom economy.","","Double the correct value; divide by total Mr of both reactants."]}
{"id":"ch-008","s":"chem","t":"3.1.2","lv":2,"q":"A compound contains 40.0% C, 6.7% H and 53.3% O by mass and has Mr = 180.0. What is its molecular formula? (H 1.0, C 12.0, O 16.0)","o":["C₆H₁₂O₆","CH₂O","C₃H₆O₃","C₁₂H₂₄O₁₂"],"a":0,"x":"Moles ratio C : H : O = 3.33 : 6.7 : 3.33 = 1 : 2 : 1, so the empirical formula is CH₂O (Mr 30.0). 180.0 ÷ 30.0 = 6, giving C₆H₁₂O₆.","w":["","This is the empirical formula (Mr 30.0); multiply by 180.0 ÷ 30.0 = 6.","Multiplied by 3 not 6; C₃H₆O₃ has Mr 90.0, not 180.0.","Multiplied by 12; C₁₂H₂₄O₁₂ has Mr 360.0, double the given 180.0."]}
{"id":"ch-009","s":"chem","t":"3.1.2","lv":2,"q":"25.0 cm³ of NaOH(aq) is neutralised by 20.0 cm³ of 0.100 mol dm⁻³ H₂SO₄. What is the concentration of the NaOH?","o":["0.160 mol dm⁻³","0.0800 mol dm⁻³","0.125 mol dm⁻³","0.320 mol dm⁻³"],"a":0,"x":"n(H₂SO₄) = 0.100 × 0.0200 = 2.00 × 10⁻³ mol. H₂SO₄ + 2NaOH, so n(NaOH) = 4.00 × 10⁻³ mol. Concentration = 4.00 × 10⁻³ ÷ 0.0250 = 0.160 mol dm⁻³.","w":["","Forgot the 2:1 ratio; H₂SO₄ gives twice as many moles of NaOH.","Inverted the volumes and ignored the 2:1 ratio in the equation.","Doubled twice; use the 2:1 ratio just once."]}
{"id":"ch-010","s":"chem","t":"3.1.2","lv":2,"q":"10.0 g of CaCO₃ is heated and 4.20 g of CaO is obtained (CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂). What is the percentage yield? (Ca 40.1, C 12.0, O 16.0)","o":["42.0%","133%","56.0%","74.9%"],"a":3,"x":"Mr CaCO₃ = 100.1, so 0.0999 mol gives a theoretical 0.0999 × 56.1 = 5.60 g CaO. Percentage yield = 4.20 ÷ 5.60 × 100 = 74.9%.","w":["Divided actual mass by starting mass (4.20 ÷ 10.0), ignoring moles and Mr.","Inverted the fraction: theoretical ÷ actual gives over 100%.","This is 5.60 ÷ 10.0, theoretical over starting mass; use 4.20 ÷ 5.60.",""]}
{"id":"ch-011","s":"chem","t":"3.1.2","lv":3,"q":"0.250 g of a Group 2 metal reacts with excess HCl, giving 255 cm³ of H₂ at 298 K and 100 kPa. Using pV = nRT, which metal is it? (R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹; Ar: Be 9.0, Mg 24.3, Ca 40.1, Sr 87.6)","o":["Magnesium","Beryllium","Calcium","Strontium"],"a":0,"x":"V = 2.55 × 10⁻⁴ m³ and p = 1.00 × 10⁵ Pa, so n(H₂) = pV ÷ RT = 0.01030 mol. M + 2HCl → MCl₂ + H₂, so n(metal) = n(H₂) and Ar = 0.250 ÷ 0.01030 = 24.3: magnesium.","w":["","Ar 9.0 would need about 0.028 mol H₂; the measured 0.0103 mol gives Ar 24.3.","Ar 40.1 would give only 0.0062 mol H₂; check n = pV ÷ RT and Ar = mass ÷ n.","Ar 87.6 is far too high; the H₂ moles give Ar of about 24.3."]}
{"id":"ch-012","s":"chem","t":"3.1.3","lv":1,"q":"Which species contains a dative covalent bond?","o":["NH₃","CH₄","MgCl₂","NH₄⁺"],"a":3,"x":"In NH₄⁺ the N atom donates its lone pair to an H⁺ ion, so both electrons in that N–H bond come from nitrogen. NH₃ and CH₄ have only ordinary covalent bonds.","w":["NH₃ has a lone pair but all its bonds are ordinary covalent bonds.","CH₄ has no lone pairs, so no dative bond is possible.","MgCl₂ is ionic, so it has no covalent bonds at all.",""]}
{"id":"ch-013","s":"chem","t":"3.1.3","lv":1,"q":"What is the H–N–H bond angle in a molecule of ammonia, NH₃?","o":["107°","109.5°","104.5°","120°"],"a":0,"x":"NH₃ has three bonding pairs and one lone pair. The lone pair repels more strongly than a bonding pair, squeezing the angle from 109.5° to 107°.","w":["","Forgot the lone pair; 109.5° is for four equal bonding pairs, as in CH₄.","Mixed up with water, which has two lone pairs and so is squeezed to 104.5°.","Trigonal planar angle; NH₃ has a lone pair so is pyramidal, not flat."]}
{"id":"ch-014","s":"chem","t":"3.1.3","lv":1,"q":"Which molecule contains polar bonds but is non-polar overall?","o":["CHCl₃","CCl₄","NH₃","H₂O"],"a":1,"x":"CCl₄ is tetrahedral and symmetrical, so the four C–Cl dipoles cancel. In CHCl₃ the C–H and C–Cl dipoles differ, so they do not cancel; pyramidal NH₃ and bent H₂O are also polar.","w":["C–H and C–Cl dipoles differ, so they do not cancel and CHCl₃ is polar.","","Pyramidal shape means the N–H dipoles do not cancel, so NH₃ is polar.","Bent shape means the O–H dipoles do not cancel, so H₂O is polar."]}
{"id":"ch-015","s":"chem","t":"3.1.3","lv":1,"q":"Why does water have a higher boiling point than hydrogen sulfide, H₂S?","o":["Hydrogen bonds form between water molecules","O–H covalent bonds are stronger than S–H bonds and must be broken","Water molecules have more electrons, so London forces are stronger","Water molecules are larger than H₂S molecules"],"a":0,"x":"Oxygen is small, very electronegative and has lone pairs, so water forms hydrogen bonds. Boiling breaks intermolecular forces, not covalent bonds. H₂S has more electrons.","w":["","Boiling breaks weak intermolecular forces, not covalent bonds within molecules.","H₂S has more electrons, so its London forces are stronger, not water's.","Water is smaller than H₂S, and size alone doesn't explain the difference."]}
{"id":"ch-016","s":"chem","t":"3.1.3","lv":2,"q":"Why does magnesium have a higher melting point than sodium?","o":["Mg atoms have a greater mass, so there are stronger London forces","Mg has two outer electrons, so it forms stronger covalent bonds","Mg²⁺ is smaller and more highly charged, and gives two delocalised electrons","Mg has more neutrons, so its ions are held together more tightly"],"a":2,"x":"Metallic bonding is the attraction between cations and delocalised electrons. Mg²⁺ has a higher charge, a smaller radius and gives two electrons per atom, so the attraction is stronger.","w":["Metals have no London forces between atoms; they have metallic bonding.","Metals do not form covalent bonds; they have cations and delocalised electrons.","","Neutrons add no charge, so they don't strengthen metallic bonding."]}
{"id":"ch-017","s":"chem","t":"3.1.3","lv":3,"q":"XeF₄ has four bonding pairs and two lone pairs around the Xe atom. What is its shape and F–Xe–F bond angle?","o":["Tetrahedral, 109.5°","Square planar, 90°","Seesaw, 90° and 120°","Octahedral, 90°"],"a":1,"x":"Six electron pairs arrange octahedrally. The two lone pairs sit opposite each other, to minimise repulsion, leaving the four F atoms in a flat square with 90° angles.","w":["That shape is for four pairs only; XeF₄ has six pairs including two lone pairs.","","Seesaw has one lone pair among five pairs, not two lone pairs among six.","Octahedral is the pair arrangement; with two lone pairs the shape is square planar."]}
{"id":"ch-018","s":"chem","t":"3.1.4","lv":1,"q":"Which equation represents the standard enthalpy of formation of methane?","o":["C(graphite) + 2H₂(g) → CH₄(g)","C(g) + 4H(g) → CH₄(g)","C(graphite) + 4H(g) → CH₄(g)","2C(graphite) + 4H₂(g) → 2CH₄(g)"],"a":0,"x":"Formation is 1 mol of the compound made from its elements in their standard states: carbon as graphite and hydrogen as H₂(g). Gaseous atoms are not standard states.","w":["","Starts from gaseous atoms; standard states are graphite and H₂(g).","Carbon is right but hydrogen must be H₂(g), not H atoms.","Makes 2 mol of CH₄; formation must be for exactly 1 mol of compound."]}
{"id":"ch-019","s":"chem","t":"3.1.4","lv":2,"q":"Burning 1.15 g of ethanol (Mr = 46.0) raises the temperature of 200 g of water by 14.0 °C. c = 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹. What is the enthalpy of combustion?","o":["+468 kJ mol⁻¹","−11.7 kJ mol⁻¹","−468 kJ mol⁻¹","−2.69 kJ mol⁻¹"],"a":2,"x":"q = mcΔT = 200 × 4.18 × 14.0 = 11 704 J = 11.7 kJ. n(ethanol) = 1.15 ÷ 46.0 = 0.0250 mol. ΔH = −11.7 ÷ 0.0250 = −468 kJ mol⁻¹, negative because the water warms up.","w":["Sign slip: the water warms, so combustion is exothermic and ΔH is negative.","Forgot to divide by moles; this is just q in kJ for 1.15 g of ethanol.","","Too small: 11.7 kJ ÷ 0.0250 mol gives 468 kJ mol⁻¹, not 2.69."]}
{"id":"ch-020","s":"chem","t":"3.1.4","lv":2,"q":"Given ΔfH(CaCO₃) = −1207, ΔfH(CaO) = −635 and ΔfH(CO₂) = −394 kJ mol⁻¹, what is ΔH for CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂?","o":["+178 kJ mol⁻¹","−178 kJ mol⁻¹","+966 kJ mol⁻¹","−2236 kJ mol⁻¹"],"a":0,"x":"ΔH = ΣΔfH(products) − ΣΔfH(reactants) = (−635 − 394) − (−1207) = +178 kJ mol⁻¹. Reversing the subtraction gives the sign error −178.","w":["","Reversed products minus reactants, giving the wrong sign.","Took ΔfH(CO₂) as +394; both product values are negative: −635 − 394.","Added all three ΔfH values instead of products minus reactants."]}
{"id":"ch-021","s":"chem","t":"3.1.4","lv":2,"q":"Mean bond enthalpies (kJ mol⁻¹): C–H 413, Cl–Cl 243, C–Cl 346, H–Cl 432. What is ΔH for CH₄ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + HCl?","o":["+122 kJ mol⁻¹","−365 kJ mol⁻¹","−122 kJ mol⁻¹","−778 kJ mol⁻¹"],"a":2,"x":"Bonds broken: C–H + Cl–Cl = 413 + 243 = 656. Bonds made: C–Cl + H–Cl = 346 + 432 = 778. ΔH = broken − made = 656 − 778 = −122 kJ mol⁻¹.","w":["Reversed the subtraction (made − broken) giving the wrong sign.","Left out a bond broken: only the C–H was counted, not the Cl–Cl.","","Only counted bonds made and forgot to subtract bonds broken."]}
{"id":"ch-022","s":"chem","t":"3.1.4","lv":3,"q":"ΔcH (kJ mol⁻¹): C(s) = −394, H₂(g) = −286, C₃H₈(g) = −2219. What is the standard enthalpy of formation of propane, C₃H₈(g)?","o":["+107 kJ mol⁻¹","+1539 kJ mol⁻¹","−4545 kJ mol⁻¹","−107 kJ mol⁻¹"],"a":3,"x":"3C + 4H₂ → C₃H₈. ΔfH = ΣΔcH(reactants) − ΣΔcH(products) = [3(−394) + 4(−286)] − (−2219) = −2326 + 2219 = −107 kJ mol⁻¹.","w":["Sign slip: reactants minus products was reversed.","Ignored the 3 and 4 in 3C + 4H₂ and reversed the subtraction.","Added the combustion values instead of subtracting product from reactants.",""]}
{"id":"ch-023","s":"chem","t":"3.1.5","lv":1,"q":"How does a catalyst increase the rate of a reaction?","o":["It provides an alternative route with a lower activation energy","It increases the energy of every collision","It increases the enthalpy change so more product forms","It increases the number of reactant molecules present"],"a":0,"x":"A catalyst offers an alternative pathway with a lower activation energy, so a greater proportion of collisions are successful at the same temperature.","w":["","Catalysts do not raise collision energy; they lower the activation energy needed.","Catalysts do not change ΔH or the amount of product at equilibrium.","Catalysts do not change how many reactant molecules are present."]}
{"id":"ch-024","s":"chem","t":"3.1.5","lv":2,"q":"The temperature of a gas mixture is raised. Which statement about the Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution is correct?","o":["The peak moves to lower energy and is higher; the area stays the same","The peak moves to higher energy and is lower; the area increases","The activation energy moves to a lower energy on the axis","The peak moves to higher energy and is lower; the area stays the same"],"a":3,"x":"Heating shifts the peak right and flattens it. The area equals the total number of molecules, which does not change, and Ea stays put, so more molecules have E ≥ Ea.","w":["That's cooling; heating moves the peak to higher energy.","Area is the total number of molecules, which stays fixed when heated.","Ea is unchanged by temperature; only a catalyst lowers it.",""]}
{"id":"ch-025","s":"chem","t":"3.1.5","lv":3,"q":"A 10 K rise from 298 K roughly doubles the rate of a reaction. What is the main reason?","o":["The collision frequency doubles","The activation energy is halved","The concentration of the reactants increases","A much larger proportion of collisions have energy ≥ the activation energy"],"a":3,"x":"Collision frequency rises by less than 2% for a 10 K rise. The exponential increase in the fraction of molecules with E ≥ Ea is what roughly doubles the rate.","w":["Collision frequency rises by only about 2% for 10 K, so it does not double.","Activation energy is fixed for a reaction; heating does not halve it.","Heating does not change concentration; it changes the energy of the particles.",""]}
{"id":"ch-026","s":"chem","t":"3.1.6","lv":1,"q":"N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), ΔH = −92 kJ mol⁻¹. Which change moves the equilibrium position to the right?","o":["Increasing the temperature","Decreasing the pressure","Adding an iron catalyst","Increasing the pressure"],"a":3,"x":"There are 4 mol of gas on the left and 2 mol on the right. Higher pressure favours the side with fewer gas moles. Heating favours the endothermic (left) direction. A catalyst does not shift the position.","w":["The forward reaction is exothermic, so heating favours the left, not the right.","Lower pressure favours the side with more gas moles, which is the left.","A catalyst speeds up both directions equally, so the position does not move.",""]}
{"id":"ch-027","s":"chem","t":"3.1.6","lv":1,"q":"What are the units of Kc for 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g)?","o":["mol dm⁻³","mol⁻² dm⁶","mol⁻¹ dm³","no units"],"a":2,"x":"Kc = [SO₃]² ÷ ([SO₂]²[O₂]). Units: (mol dm⁻³)² ÷ (mol dm⁻³)³ = (mol dm⁻³)⁻¹ = mol⁻¹ dm³.","w":["Wrong power: there are 2 concentration terms on top and 3 below, so the power is −1.","That power (−2) fits N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃; here the net power is −1, not −2.","","Units only vanish if the terms cancel fully; here there are 3 below and only 2 above."]}
{"id":"ch-028","s":"chem","t":"3.1.6","lv":1,"q":"The forward reaction of an equilibrium is exothermic. What happens when the temperature is increased?","o":["Kc increases and the position shifts to the right","Kc is unchanged and the position shifts to the left","Kc decreases and the position shifts to the left","Kc is unchanged and the position shifts to the right"],"a":2,"x":"The system opposes the rise by favouring the endothermic (reverse) reaction. The equilibrium position moves left and Kc, which depends only on temperature, decreases.","w":["Heating an exothermic reaction does not favour products, so Kc falls and it shifts left.","Kc does change with temperature; it is the only thing that changes it.","","Kc does change with temperature, and heating an exothermic reaction shifts it left."]}
{"id":"ch-029","s":"chem","t":"3.1.6","lv":3,"q":"2.00 mol of A and 2.00 mol of B are mixed in 2.00 dm³ at constant T: A + B ⇌ C. At equilibrium there is 1.20 mol of C. What is Kc?","o":["1.88 dm³ mol⁻¹","3.75 dm³ mol⁻¹","0.267 mol dm⁻³","0.938 dm³ mol⁻¹"],"a":1,"x":"At equilibrium A = B = 0.80 mol, C = 1.20 mol. Concentrations: 0.40, 0.40, 0.60 mol dm⁻³. Kc = 0.60 ÷ (0.40 × 0.40) = 3.75 dm³ mol⁻¹. Using moles instead of concentrations gives 1.88.","w":["Used moles instead of concentrations; the 2.00 dm³ volume does not cancel here.","","Inverted the expression (reactants ÷ product); it should be [C] ÷ ([A][B]).","Mixed moles and concentrations; use concentrations for every species."]}
{"id":"ch-030","s":"chem","t":"3.1.7","lv":1,"q":"What is the oxidation state of oxygen in hydrogen peroxide, H₂O₂?","o":["−2","−1","0","+1"],"a":1,"x":"Hydrogen is +1, and the molecule is neutral, so the two oxygens total −2, giving −1 each. Peroxides are an exception to the usual −2 for oxygen.","w":["−2 is the usual oxygen value, but peroxides are an exception.","","Oxygen is 0 only in the element; with H at +1, each O in H₂O₂ must be −1.","+1 is hydrogen's value; the two oxygens must balance to give −1 each."]}
{"id":"ch-031","s":"chem","t":"3.1.7","lv":2,"q":"In Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂, which statement is correct?","o":["Fe₂O₃ is the reducing agent because iron goes from +3 to 0","Carbon is reduced because it goes from +2 to +4","Oxygen is oxidised because it is lost from Fe₂O₃","CO is the reducing agent because carbon goes from +2 to +4"],"a":3,"x":"Carbon's oxidation state rises from +2 to +4, so CO is oxidised and is the reducing agent. Iron falls from +3 to 0, so it is reduced and Fe₂O₃ is the oxidising agent.","w":["Iron falls from +3 to 0, so it is reduced; Fe₂O₃ is the oxidising agent.","Carbon goes from +2 to +4, which is oxidation, not reduction.","Oxygen stays at −2 throughout; the iron is reduced, not the oxygen oxidised.",""]}
{"id":"ch-032","s":"chem","t":"3.1.7","lv":2,"q":"Which is the correct half-equation for the reduction of dichromate(VI) ions in acid?","o":["Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O + 6e⁻","Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O","Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 3e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O","Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 7H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O"],"a":1,"x":"Each Cr goes from +6 to +3, so 6 electrons are gained, on the left. Check: charge on left is −2 + 14 − 6 = +6, and on right 2 × 3+ = +6. 14 H⁺ balance the 7 H₂O.","w":["Electrons are on the wrong side; that would be oxidation, and reduction gains electrons.","","Only 3 electrons: each Cr goes from +6 to +3, so 6 electrons are needed in all.","Only 7 H⁺ do not balance 7 H₂O, and the charges do not balance either."]}
{"id":"ch-033","s":"chem","t":"3.1.7","lv":3,"q":"MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O and Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ + e⁻. How many moles of Fe²⁺ are oxidised by 20.0 cm³ of 0.0200 mol dm⁻³ MnO₄⁻?","o":["4.00 × 10⁻⁴ mol","8.00 × 10⁻⁵ mol","2.00 × 10⁻³ mol","2.00 × 10⁻² mol"],"a":2,"x":"n(MnO₄⁻) = 0.0200 × 0.0200 = 4.00 × 10⁻⁴ mol. Each MnO₄⁻ accepts 5 electrons, and each Fe²⁺ gives 1, so n(Fe²⁺) = 5 × 4.00 × 10⁻⁴ = 2.00 × 10⁻³ mol.","w":["Forgot the 1:5 ratio; this is just n(MnO₄⁻), not n(Fe²⁺).","Divided by 5 instead of multiplying; each MnO₄⁻ takes 5 electrons so 5 Fe²⁺.","","Power-of-ten slip when converting 20.0 cm³ to dm³; the answer is 2.00 × 10⁻³."]}
{"id":"ch-034","s":"chem","t":"3.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Why does atomic radius decrease across Period 3 from sodium to chlorine?","o":["Shielding increases while nuclear charge stays the same","The number of electron shells decreases","Nuclear charge increases while shielding stays about the same","Extra electrons are added to the inner shells"],"a":2,"x":"Across a period, electrons go into the same shell, so shielding is almost constant. The increasing nuclear charge pulls the outer electrons in more strongly.","w":["Shielding stays about the same across a period, and nuclear charge does increase.","The number of shells stays the same across the period; it does not decrease.","","Electrons are added to the same outer shell, not to inner shells."]}
{"id":"ch-035","s":"chem","t":"3.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Why is the first ionisation energy of sulfur lower than that of phosphorus?","o":["S has a smaller nuclear charge than P","S has its outer electron in a 3d sub-shell","In S, two electrons share a 3p orbital and repel each other","S has more electron shells than P"],"a":2,"x":"P has 3p³, with each electron in its own orbital. S has 3p⁴, where one orbital holds a pair. Repulsion between the paired electrons makes one easier to remove.","w":["S has a larger nuclear charge than P, so this does not explain the drop.","The outer electrons of S are in 3p, not 3d; 3d is not filled in Period 3.","","S and P are in the same period, so both have the same number of shells."]}
{"id":"ch-036","s":"chem","t":"3.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Why does sulfur (S₈) have a higher melting point than white phosphorus (P₄)?","o":["S–S covalent bonds are stronger than P–P bonds","Sulfur has a giant covalent structure","S₈ molecules are polar, so have permanent dipole forces","S₈ molecules are larger, so there are stronger London forces"],"a":3,"x":"Both are simple molecular, so melting breaks only intermolecular forces. S₈ has more electrons than P₄, so the London forces between molecules are stronger.","w":["Covalent bonds inside the molecules are not broken when simple molecular solids melt.","S₈ is simple molecular; a giant covalent solid would melt far higher.","Both are non-polar, so neither has permanent dipole forces.",""]}
{"id":"ch-037","s":"chem","t":"3.2.2","lv":1,"q":"Why does reactivity increase down Group 2?","o":["Atoms are larger with more shielding, so outer electrons are lost more easily","Nuclear charge decreases, so outer electrons are lost more easily","Atomic radius decreases, so outer electrons are lost more easily","Electronegativity increases, so atoms attract electrons more strongly"],"a":0,"x":"Down the group the outer electrons are further from the nucleus and more shielded. Less energy is needed to remove them, so the ionisation energies fall and reactivity rises.","w":["","Nuclear charge increases down the group; the extra shielding outweighs it.","Atomic radius increases down the group, not decreases.","Electronegativity falls down the group, and this isn't about gaining electrons."]}
{"id":"ch-038","s":"chem","t":"3.2.2","lv":2,"q":"Magnesium is heated in steam and burns with a bright white light. Which equation represents this reaction?","o":["Mg(s) + 2H₂O(g) → Mg(OH)₂(s) + H₂(g)","Mg(s) + H₂O(g) → MgO(s) + H₂(g)","Mg(s) + H₂O(g) → MgO(s) + O₂(g)","2Mg(s) + H₂O(g) → Mg₂O(s) + H₂(g)"],"a":1,"x":"In steam, magnesium burns to give white magnesium oxide and hydrogen. Mg(OH)₂ is the product with cold water, which reacts very slowly. The oxide contains Mg²⁺ and O²⁻, so it is MgO, and no O₂ is made.","w":["Mg(OH)₂ is the slow cold-water product; steam gives the oxide.","","Hydrogen is released from water, not oxygen.","Magnesium ions are Mg²⁺, so the oxide is MgO, not Mg₂O."]}
{"id":"ch-039","s":"chem","t":"3.2.2","lv":2,"q":"1.85 g of Ca(OH)₂ (Mr = 74.1) is neutralised by 0.500 mol dm⁻³ HCl. What volume of acid is needed?","o":["99.9 cm³","49.9 cm³","130 cm³","200 cm³"],"a":0,"x":"n(Ca(OH)₂) = 1.85 ÷ 74.1 = 0.02497 mol. Ca(OH)₂ + 2HCl, so n(HCl) = 0.04994 mol. V = 0.04994 ÷ 0.500 = 0.0999 dm³ = 99.9 cm³.","w":["","Forgot the 1:2 ratio; each Ca(OH)₂ needs 2 HCl, so double the moles.","Used Mr 57.1 (CaOH); Ca(OH)₂ has two OH groups, so Mr is 74.1.","Used 4 HCl per Ca(OH)₂ instead of 2."]}
{"id":"ch-040","s":"chem","t":"3.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Chlorine water is added to aqueous potassium bromide. What is observed, and what is the ionic equation?","o":["The solution turns orange; Br₂ + 2Cl⁻ → 2Br⁻ + Cl₂","A cream precipitate forms; Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂","There is no reaction, as chlorine is less reactive than bromine","The solution turns orange; Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂"],"a":3,"x":"Chlorine is a stronger oxidising agent than bromine, so it oxidises Br⁻ ions to Br₂, which turns the solution orange. No precipitate forms: a cream precipitate is the silver nitrate test for Br⁻.","w":["Reversed the equation; chlorine oxidises bromide, not the other way.","A cream precipitate is the AgNO₃ test, not the chlorine displacement.","Chlorine is more reactive than bromine, so it does displace it.",""]}
{"id":"ch-041","s":"chem","t":"3.2.3","lv":1,"q":"A halide solution is acidified with dilute nitric acid and silver nitrate is added. A cream precipitate forms, which dissolves in concentrated NH₃ but not in dilute NH₃. Which halide ion is present?","o":["Cl⁻","Br⁻","I⁻","F⁻"],"a":1,"x":"AgBr is cream and dissolves only in concentrated ammonia. AgCl is white and dissolves in dilute ammonia, AgI is yellow and insoluble, and AgF is soluble so gives no precipitate.","w":["AgCl is white and dissolves in dilute NH₃.","","AgI is yellow and insoluble in even concentrated NH₃.","AgF is soluble, so no precipitate forms."]}
{"id":"ch-042","s":"chem","t":"3.2.3","lv":2,"q":"Solid sodium iodide reacts with concentrated sulfuric acid. Which product has a smell of bad eggs?","o":["SO₂","HI","I₂","H₂S"],"a":3,"x":"Iodide ions are strong enough reducing agents to reduce sulfur from +6 to −2, forming H₂S. SO₂ is choking, I₂ is a purple vapour or black solid, and HI is a colourless gas.","w":["SO₂ is made, but it has a choking smell, not bad eggs.","HI forms as steamy fumes, but it doesn't smell of bad eggs.","I₂ is a purple vapour or black solid, with no bad egg smell.",""]}
{"id":"ch-043","s":"chem","t":"3.2.3","lv":2,"q":"Chlorine reacts with cold, dilute aqueous NaOH: Cl₂ + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaClO + H₂O. What happens to the chlorine?","o":["It is reduced only, from 0 to −1","It is oxidised only, from 0 to +1","It disproportionates, from 0 to −1 and +5","It disproportionates, from 0 to −1 and +1"],"a":3,"x":"In NaCl chlorine is −1 (reduced) and in NaClO it is +1 (oxidised), so the same element is both oxidised and reduced. The +5 state forms with hot NaOH.","w":["Ignores NaClO, where chlorine is +1, so it is also oxidised.","Ignores NaCl, where chlorine is −1, so it is also reduced.","The +5 state (ClO₃⁻) forms with hot NaOH, not cold.",""]}
{"id":"ch-044","s":"chem","t":"3.3.1","lv":1,"q":"What is the IUPAC name of CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃?","o":["3-methylbutane","2-methylpropane","2-methylbutane","2-ethylpropane"],"a":2,"x":"The longest chain is four carbons (butane). A CH₃ branch is on carbon 2 when numbering from the end that gives the lower number, so it is 2-methylbutane.","w":["Wrong numbering; the methyl group is on carbon 2, not 3.","2-methylpropane has 4 carbons; this molecule has 5, on a four-carbon chain.","","Picked a three-carbon chain; the longest chain is four carbons."]}
{"id":"ch-045","s":"chem","t":"3.3.1","lv":2,"q":"Which compound shows E/Z isomerism?","o":["But-1-ene","2-methylbut-2-ene","Propene","Pent-2-ene"],"a":3,"x":"E/Z needs two different groups on each carbon of C=C. Pent-2-ene has H and CH₃ on one carbon and H and C₂H₅ on the other. The others each have a carbon with two identical groups.","w":["The C1 end has two H atoms, so no E/Z isomerism.","Carbon 2 has two CH₃ groups, so no E/Z isomerism.","One carbon has two H atoms, so no E/Z isomerism.",""]}
{"id":"ch-046","s":"chem","t":"3.3.1","lv":1,"q":"What is formed when the Cl–Cl bond undergoes homolytic fission?","o":["A Cl⁺ ion and a Cl⁻ ion","Two chlorine radicals, each with one unpaired electron","Two Cl⁻ ions","Two Cl⁺ ions"],"a":1,"x":"In homolytic fission the bonding pair is shared equally, one electron going to each atom. This gives two radicals. Heterolytic fission gives a pair of ions.","w":["Confused with heterolytic fission, which gives one positive and one negative ion.","","Homolytic fission gives neutral radicals; Cl⁺ ions would need lost electrons.",""]}
{"id":"ch-047","s":"chem","t":"3.3.1","lv":3,"q":"How many different alkenes, including E/Z isomers, have the molecular formula C₄H₈?","o":["2","3","4","5"],"a":2,"x":"But-1-ene, methylpropene, (E)-but-2-ene and (Z)-but-2-ene. There are 3 structural isomers, but but-2-ene exists as two stereoisomers, so the total is 4.","w":["Only counted but-1-ene and but-2-ene, ignoring methylpropene and E/Z.","Counted structural isomers only and forgot E/Z for but-2-ene.","","Overcounted, perhaps adding cyclobutane, which is not an alkene."]}
{"id":"ch-048","s":"chem","t":"3.3.2","lv":1,"q":"Which describes the molecules that leave near the top of a fractionating column?","o":["Short chains, with strong London forces and low boiling points","Short chains, with weak London forces and low boiling points","Long chains, with strong London forces and high boiling points","Long chains, with weak London forces and low boiling points"],"a":1,"x":"Short molecules have fewer electrons and weaker London forces, so they have low boiling points and stay as vapour up to the cooler top of the column.","w":["Short chains have weak, not strong, London forces.","","This describes the bottom of the column, where long chains condense.","Long chains have stronger London forces and higher boiling points."]}
{"id":"ch-049","s":"chem","t":"3.3.2","lv":1,"q":"Which equation correctly represents the thermal cracking of decane?","o":["C₁₀H₂₂ → C₆H₁₄ + 2C₂H₄","C₁₀H₂₂ → C₆H₁₂ + 2C₂H₄","C₁₀H₂₂ → C₆H₁₄ + C₂H₄","C₁₀H₂₂ → C₇H₁₆ + 2C₂H₄"],"a":0,"x":"Count atoms. Right side: C = 6 + 4 = 10, H = 14 + 8 = 22. The other options give 20 H, 8 C and 11 C respectively.","w":["","Hydrogen slip: C₆H₁₂ + 2C₂H₄ has only 20 H, not 22.","One C₂H₄ gives only 8 C and 18 H, so neither element balances.","C₇H₁₆ + 2C₂H₄ gives 11 C and 24 H, so atoms are created from nowhere."]}
{"id":"ch-050","s":"chem","t":"3.3.2","lv":2,"q":"Which equation shows a propagation step in the chlorination of methane?","o":["CH₃· + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + Cl·","Cl₂ → 2Cl·","CH₃· + Cl· → CH₃Cl","2CH₃· → C₂H₆"],"a":0,"x":"In propagation a radical reacts and a new radical is made. Cl₂ → 2Cl· is initiation. The other two options use up two radicals and make none, so they are termination steps.","w":["","This is initiation: Cl₂ splits into radicals and no radical is used up.","Termination: two radicals combine and no radical is left to continue the chain.","Termination: two methyl radicals join to make ethane, leaving no radical."]}
{"id":"ch-051","s":"chem","t":"3.3.3","lv":1,"q":"Which reagent and conditions convert 1-bromopropane into propan-1-ol?","o":["Ethanolic KOH, heat under reflux","Aqueous NaOH, heat under reflux","Concentrated H₂SO₄, heat","Acidified K₂Cr₂O₇, heat under reflux"],"a":1,"x":"Aqueous hydroxide ions act as nucleophiles and substitute the bromine. Ethanolic KOH causes elimination to propene instead.","w":["Ethanolic KOH favours elimination to propene, not substitution to the alcohol.","","Conc. H₂SO₄ dehydrates alcohols; it doesn't substitute a halogenoalkane.","Acidified dichromate oxidises alcohols; it can't make one from a halogenoalkane."]}
{"id":"ch-052","s":"chem","t":"3.3.3","lv":2,"q":"2-bromopropane is heated with ethanolic KOH. Which describes the reaction?","o":["Nucleophilic substitution, with OH⁻ acting as a nucleophile to form propan-2-ol","Electrophilic addition, with OH⁻ acting as an electrophile to form propene","Elimination, with OH⁻ acting as a nucleophile to form propene","Elimination, with OH⁻ acting as a base to form propene"],"a":3,"x":"Ethanolic conditions favour elimination. OH⁻ removes an H⁺ from a carbon next to C–Br, so it acts as a base, and HBr is lost to give propene.","w":["Those are aqueous conditions; ethanolic KOH favours elimination, not substitution.","Making an alkene here is elimination, not addition, and OH⁻ is never an electrophile.","Right reaction, but OH⁻ removes an H⁺ here, so it acts as a base, not a nucleophile.",""]}
{"id":"ch-053","s":"chem","t":"3.3.3","lv":2,"q":"Which equation shows chlorine radicals being regenerated during the breakdown of ozone?","o":["Cl· + O₃ → ClO· + O₂","CF₂Cl₂ → CF₂Cl· + Cl·","ClO· + O₃ → Cl· + 2O₂","Cl· + Cl· → Cl₂"],"a":2,"x":"Cl· is used up in Cl· + O₃ → ClO· + O₂, then regenerated in ClO· + O₃ → Cl· + 2O₂. This makes it a catalyst, so one radical can destroy many ozone molecules.","w":["This uses up Cl· to make ClO·; Cl· is regenerated only in the next step.","This is initiation: UV breaks a C–Cl bond in the CFC to make Cl· in the first place.","","This is termination: two Cl· combine and the radicals are removed."]}
{"id":"ch-054","s":"chem","t":"3.3.4","lv":1,"q":"What is observed when an alkene is shaken with bromine water?","o":["Orange to green","Colourless to orange","Orange to colourless","Orange to purple"],"a":2,"x":"Bromine adds across the C=C double bond, forming a colourless dibromoalkane, so the orange bromine water is decolourised.","w":["Orange to green is the dichromate oxidation test, not bromine water.","Backwards: bromine water starts orange and loses its colour.","","Nothing purple forms; bromine adds across C=C and the colour is simply lost."]}
{"id":"ch-055","s":"chem","t":"3.3.4","lv":2,"q":"What is the major product when propene reacts with HBr, and why?","o":["2-bromopropane, via a more stable secondary carbocation","1-bromopropane, via a more stable primary carbocation","2-bromopropane, via a more stable primary carbocation","1-bromopropane, via a more stable secondary carbocation"],"a":0,"x":"H⁺ adds to the end carbon, giving the secondary carbocation CH₃CH⁺CH₃. Alkyl groups push electron density towards the positive carbon, so a secondary carbocation is more stable than a primary one.","w":["","Wrong product and wrong idea: a primary carbocation is less stable than a secondary.","Right product, but the carbocation involved is secondary, not primary.","Secondary carbocation is right, but it leads to 2-bromopropane, not 1-bromopropane."]}
{"id":"ch-056","s":"chem","t":"3.3.4","lv":1,"q":"A section of an addition polymer is –CH₂–CHCl–CH₂–CHCl–CH₂–CHCl–. What is the monomer?","o":["CH₂=CHCl","ClCH=CHCl","CH₂=CCl₂","CH₃CH₂Cl"],"a":0,"x":"The chain repeats –CH₂–CHCl–. Opening the C=C bond of CH₂=CHCl (chloroethene) and joining many units gives this backbone of single bonds.","w":["","ClCH=CHCl would give –CHCl–CHCl– repeat units, with Cl on every carbon.","CH₂=CCl₂ would give –CH₂–CCl₂– units, with two Cl on one carbon.","CH₃CH₂Cl is saturated, with no C=C, so it can't form an addition polymer."]}
{"id":"ch-057","s":"chem","t":"3.3.5","lv":1,"q":"Which conditions are used to ferment glucose to ethanol?","o":["Yeast, about 35 °C, excess air","Concentrated H₃PO₄, 300 °C, 60 atm","Nickel catalyst, 150 °C, hydrogen","Yeast, about 35 °C, no oxygen"],"a":3,"x":"Yeast enzymes convert glucose to ethanol and CO₂ at about 30–40 °C. Oxygen must be excluded, otherwise the ethanol is oxidised to ethanoic acid.","w":["Excess air oxidises ethanol to ethanoic acid, so oxygen must be excluded.","These are the conditions for hydrating ethene industrially, not fermentation.","Nickel and hydrogen is hydrogenation of alkenes, not fermentation.",""]}
{"id":"ch-058","s":"chem","t":"3.3.5","lv":2,"q":"Ethanol is heated under reflux with excess acidified potassium dichromate(VI). What is the product and colour change?","o":["Ethanal; orange to green","Ethanoic acid; orange to green","Ethanoic acid; green to orange","Ethanal; green to orange"],"a":1,"x":"Reflux with excess oxidising agent oxidises a primary alcohol fully to the carboxylic acid. Cr₂O₇²⁻ (orange) is reduced to Cr³⁺ (green). Ethanal requires distilling off as it forms.","w":["Ethanal forms only if distilled off; reflux with excess oxidant goes on to the acid.","","Right product but the colour change is reversed: dichromate goes orange to green.","Ethanal is the distillation product, and the colour change is reversed too."]}
{"id":"ch-059","s":"chem","t":"3.3.5","lv":3,"q":"Butan-2-ol is dehydrated with concentrated H₃PO₄. How many different alkene products, including stereoisomers, can form?","o":["1","3","2","4"],"a":1,"x":"Removing OH from C2 with H from C1 gives but-1-ene. Removing H from C3 gives but-2-ene, which has two stereoisomers, E and Z. That makes 3 products in total.","w":["Forgets that H can also be removed from C3, giving but-2-ene.","","Counts but-1-ene and but-2-ene but misses that but-2-ene has E and Z forms.","Overcounts: only but-1-ene and the E and Z isomers of but-2-ene can form."]}
{"id":"ch-060","s":"chem","t":"3.3.6","lv":1,"q":"In the mass spectrum of an organic compound, what does the m/z value of the molecular ion peak tell you?","o":["The number of carbon atoms in the compound","The relative molecular mass of the compound","The relative atomic mass of the heaviest atom","The mass of the most stable fragment"],"a":1,"x":"The molecular ion M⁺ is the whole molecule with one electron removed, so its m/z value equals the relative molecular mass of the compound.","w":["M⁺ gives the total mass, not the carbon count; that needs more data.","","M⁺ is the whole molecule, not the relative atomic mass of one atom.","The tallest (base) peak is often a stable fragment; M⁺ is the whole molecule."]}
{"id":"ch-061","s":"chem","t":"3.3.6","lv":3,"q":"A compound has a molecular ion peak at m/z 74. Its IR spectrum has a very broad absorption at 2500–3000 cm⁻¹ and a strong sharp absorption near 1710 cm⁻¹. What is it? (H 1.0, C 12.0, O 16.0)","o":["Propanoic acid, CH₃CH₂COOH","Methyl ethanoate, CH₃COOCH₃","Butan-1-ol, CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂OH","Propanal, CH₃CH₂CHO"],"a":0,"x":"A broad band at 2500–3000 cm⁻¹ is the O–H of a carboxylic acid, and 1710 cm⁻¹ is C=O. Propanoic acid has Mr = 74. The ester lacks the O–H band, butan-1-ol lacks C=O, and propanal has Mr = 58.","w":["","An ester also has Mr 74 and a C=O peak, but no broad O–H band at 2500–3000 cm⁻¹.","Butan-1-ol also has Mr 74, but it has no C=O peak at 1710 cm⁻¹.","Mr of propanal is 58, not 74, and an aldehyde has no broad O–H band."]}
{"id":"ch-062","s":"chem","t":"3.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement correctly describes two isotopes of the same element?","o":["Same number of protons, different number of neutrons","Same number of neutrons, different number of protons","Same number of protons and neutrons, different number of electrons","Same mass number, different number of protons"],"a":0,"x":"Isotopes are atoms of the same element, so they have the same atomic number (protons) but different mass numbers, which means different numbers of neutrons. Same mass number with different protons describes different elements.","w":["","Swapped them: isotopes have the same protons, so the neutrons must be what differ.","Different electron numbers make ions, not isotopes; the neutrons must differ.","Different protons means different elements, so these cannot be isotopes."]}
{"id":"ch-063","s":"chem","t":"3.1.2","lv":1,"q":"Which is the correct definition of relative atomic mass, Ar?","o":["Weighted mean mass of an atom compared with the mass of a carbon-12 atom","Mass of one atom compared with the mass of one hydrogen-1 atom","Mean mass of one mole of atoms of the element in grams","Weighted mean mass of an atom compared with 1/12 of the mass of a carbon-12 atom"],"a":3,"x":"Ar is a weighted mean over the isotopes, compared with 1/12 of the mass of a carbon-12 atom. Leaving out the 1/12 or using hydrogen-1 is wrong, and a mass in grams is molar mass, which has units; Ar has none.","w":["Forgot the 1/12: it is compared with 1/12 of a carbon-12 atom, not the whole atom.","Wrong standard: Ar is compared with 1/12 of a carbon-12 atom, not hydrogen-1.","This is molar mass in g mol⁻¹; Ar is a ratio with no units.",""]}
{"id":"ch-064","s":"chem","t":"3.1.1","lv":2,"q":"The first five ionisation energies of an element in kJ mol⁻¹ are 578, 1817, 2745, 11577 and 14842. In which group of the periodic table is the element?","o":["Group 2","Group 3","Group 4","Group 5"],"a":1,"x":"The big jump comes between the 3rd and 4th values. The 4th electron is taken from an inner shell, closer to the nucleus, so the element has 3 outer electrons and is in Group 3 (this is aluminium).","w":["Group 2 would show the big jump after the 2nd ionisation energy, not the 3rd.","","Group 4 would show the big jump after the 4th ionisation energy, not the 3rd.","Group 5 would show the big jump after the 5th ionisation energy, not the 3rd."]}
{"id":"ch-066","s":"chem","t":"3.1.2","lv":1,"q":"What is the empirical formula of a compound?","o":["The simplest whole number ratio of atoms of each element in the compound","The actual number of atoms of each element in one molecule of the compound","The simplest whole number ratio of the masses of each element in the compound","The formula showing which atoms are bonded to which in the molecule"],"a":0,"x":"Empirical formula is the simplest whole number ratio of atoms of each element. The actual numbers are the molecular formula, and mass ratios must be divided by Ar to get a mole ratio first.","w":["","That is the molecular formula, the actual number of atoms in one molecule.","Mass ratio is not atom ratio: divide masses by Ar to get the mole ratio first.","That describes a structural or displayed formula, not an empirical one."]}
{"id":"ch-067","s":"chem","t":"3.1.2","lv":1,"q":"A student uses pV = nRT with R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. Which pair of units should be used for p and V?","o":["p in kPa and V in m³","p in Pa and V in m³","p in Pa and V in dm³","p in kPa and V in cm³"],"a":1,"x":"R is in J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ and 1 J = 1 Pa m³, so use p in Pa and V in m³ (with T in K). Each of the other pairs makes the calculated n wrong by a factor of 1000.","w":["kPa makes pV 1000 times too small, so n comes out wrong by a factor of 1000.","","V in dm³ gives a number 1000 times too big, so n is 1000 times too big.","kPa and cm³ together are still out by a factor of 1000 compared with Pa and m³."]}
{"id":"ch-068","s":"chem","t":"3.1.2","lv":2,"q":"Which statement about a chemical reaction is correct?","o":["A reaction with 100% atom economy must have a percentage yield of 100%","Atom economy decreases when product is lost during purification","A reaction can have a percentage yield of 100% and still have a low atom economy","A low percentage yield means the reaction has a low atom economy"],"a":2,"x":"Atom economy depends only on the balanced equation (how much of the reactants ends up in the desired product). Percentage yield depends on what is actually collected. They are independent.","w":["Even with 100% atom economy, losses in the lab can still give a yield under 100%.","Losing product in purification lowers the yield, not the atom economy.","","Low yield is about what you collect; atom economy comes only from the equation."]}
{"id":"ch-069","s":"chem","t":"3.1.3","lv":1,"q":"What holds the particles together in solid sodium chloride?","o":["Shared pairs of electrons between sodium and chlorine atoms","Attraction between positive ions and delocalised electrons","London forces between NaCl molecules","Electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions"],"a":3,"x":"Sodium chloride is a giant ionic lattice of Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions held by strong electrostatic attraction. It contains no molecules, no shared pairs and no delocalised electrons.","w":["Shared pairs are covalent bonding; NaCl is ionic, with electrons transferred.","That describes metallic bonding; NaCl has no delocalised electrons.","NaCl is a giant lattice with no molecules, so there are no London forces between them.",""]}
{"id":"ch-070","s":"chem","t":"3.1.3","lv":1,"q":"Which species has a bond angle of 109.5°?","o":["NH₄⁺","NH₃","H₂O","BF₃"],"a":0,"x":"NH₄⁺ has four bonding pairs and no lone pairs, so it is tetrahedral at 109.5°. NH₃ (107°) and H₂O (104.5°) are squeezed by lone pairs, and BF₃ is trigonal planar (120°).","w":["","The lone pair on N squeezes the angle to about 107°, not 109.5°.","Two lone pairs on O squeeze the angle to 104.5°, not 109.5°.","BF₃ has three bonding pairs and no lone pairs, so it is planar at 120°."]}
{"id":"ch-071","s":"chem","t":"3.1.3","lv":2,"q":"Why does graphite conduct electricity but diamond does not?","o":["Weak forces between its layers let ions move through graphite","Each carbon uses three electrons in bonding, leaving one per atom delocalised","Graphite has positive ions in a sea of electrons, like a metal","Diamond has stronger covalent bonds, so its electrons cannot move"],"a":1,"x":"In graphite each carbon bonds to three others, leaving one electron free to move along the layers. In diamond all four outer electrons are in localised bonds. Weak forces between layers explain softness, not conduction.","w":["Weak forces between layers explain softness; graphite conducts via electrons, not ions.","","Graphite has neutral atoms, not positive ions; the delocalised electrons are the cause.","Diamond's electrons are fixed in localised bonds, not blocked by bond strength."]}
{"id":"ch-072","s":"chem","t":"3.1.3","lv":3,"q":"NH₃ and BF₃ join by a dative bond to form H₃N→BF₃. How does the shape around N and around B change?","o":["N: stays pyramidal; B: trigonal planar to tetrahedral","N: pyramidal to tetrahedral; B: stays trigonal planar","N: pyramidal to tetrahedral; B: trigonal planar to tetrahedral","N: pyramidal to tetrahedral; B: trigonal planar to pyramidal"],"a":2,"x":"N's lone pair becomes the bonding pair of the dative bond, so N goes from 3 bonding pairs and 1 lone pair (pyramidal) to 4 bonding pairs (tetrahedral). B gains a fourth bonding pair and has no lone pair, so it also becomes tetrahedral.","w":["N changes too: its lone pair becomes a bonding pair, giving four bonding pairs.","B changes too: it gains a fourth bonding pair, so it is no longer planar.","","B has no lone pair, so with four bonding pairs it is tetrahedral, not pyramidal."]}
{"id":"ch-073","s":"chem","t":"3.2.1","lv":1,"q":"Which Period 3 element has the highest melting point?","o":["Magnesium","Silicon","Aluminium","Sulfur"],"a":1,"x":"Silicon has a giant covalent lattice, so a lot of energy is needed to break its many strong covalent bonds. Magnesium and aluminium are metals with lower melting points, and sulfur is simple molecular.","w":["Magnesium is metallic, with weaker bonding than silicon's giant covalent lattice.","","Aluminium is metallic and melts at a lower temperature than silicon's covalent lattice.","Sulfur is simple molecular with weak London forces, so it melts at a low temperature."]}
{"id":"ch-074","s":"chem","t":"3.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Why is the melting point of argon much lower than that of silicon?","o":["Argon has weaker covalent bonds between its atoms than silicon","Argon atoms have a lower mass than silicon atoms","Argon has weaker metallic bonding as it has fewer delocalised electrons","Argon has weak London forces; silicon has a giant lattice of covalent bonds"],"a":3,"x":"Argon is monatomic, with only weak London forces between atoms, so little energy is needed to melt it. It has no covalent bonds between atoms, and its atoms are heavier than silicon's, not lighter.","w":["Argon is monatomic, so it has no covalent bonds at all between its atoms.","Argon atoms are heavier than silicon atoms, and mass is not the reason anyway.","Argon is a non-metal with no metallic bonding or delocalised electrons.",""]}
{"id":"ch-075","s":"chem","t":"3.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Which equation represents the first ionisation energy of magnesium?","o":["Mg(s) → Mg⁺(g) + e⁻","Mg(g) → Mg²⁺(g) + 2e⁻","Mg(g) → Mg⁺(g) + e⁻","Mg⁺(g) → Mg²⁺(g) + e⁻"],"a":2,"x":"First ionisation energy is for one mole of gaseous atoms each losing one electron: Mg(g) → Mg⁺(g) + e⁻. Mg(s) has the wrong state, Mg⁺ → Mg²⁺ is the second ionisation, and Mg → Mg²⁺ removes two electrons.","w":["Mg(s) is the wrong state; the definition needs gaseous atoms.","This removes two electrons, so it is the sum of the first two ionisations.","","Starts from Mg⁺, so this is the second ionisation energy."]}
{"id":"ch-076","s":"chem","t":"3.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Why does argon have the highest first ionisation energy in Period 3?","o":["Greatest nuclear charge in Period 3, with similar shielding","Its outer electrons are in a higher energy level than chlorine's","Its paired 3p electrons repel each other less than unpaired ones","It has more electron shells than the other Period 3 atoms"],"a":0,"x":"Across Period 3 the outer electrons stay in the third shell, so shielding is similar while nuclear charge rises. Argon has the most protons (18), so its outer electrons are held most strongly. Paired electrons repel more, not less.","w":["","Argon's outer electrons are in the same 3p subshell as chlorine's, not a higher level.","Paired electrons repel more, not less; that explains the dip, not argon's peak.","Argon has the same three shells as the other Period 3 atoms."]}
{"id":"ch-078","s":"chem","t":"3.1.1","lv":3,"q":"The second ionisation energy of sodium (4562 kJ mol⁻¹) is much greater than that of magnesium (1451 kJ mol⁻¹). What is the main reason?","o":["Na⁺ has more protons than Mg⁺, so its electrons are attracted more strongly","Sodium has stronger metallic bonding than magnesium, so more energy is needed","Na⁺ loses a 2p electron, closer to the nucleus and less shielded than 3s","Na⁺ is a larger ion than Mg⁺, so its outer electron is held more tightly"],"a":2,"x":"Na⁺ is 1s² 2s² 2p⁶, so its next electron comes from the second shell, close to the nucleus with little shielding. Mg⁺ is 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s¹, so it loses a 3s electron. Mg has the greater nuclear charge (12 against 11).","w":["Na⁺ has fewer protons (11) than Mg⁺ (12), so its nuclear charge is smaller.","Ionisation energy is for gaseous ions, so metallic bonding is irrelevant.","","Na⁺ is smaller than Mg⁺ (two shells against three), not larger."]}
{"id":"ch-079","s":"chem","t":"3.3.1","lv":1,"q":"Which statement defines structural isomers?","o":["Same structural formula but a different arrangement of atoms in space","Same empirical formula but different molecular formulae","Members of a homologous series that differ by a CH₂ unit","Same molecular formula but different structural formulae"],"a":3,"x":"Structural isomers have the same molecular formula but different structural formulae: the atoms are joined in a different order. Same structural formula with a different arrangement in space describes stereoisomers; differing by CH₂ describes a homologous series.","w":["This describes stereoisomers, which differ in arrangement in space.","Isomers share a molecular formula; different molecular formulae are not isomers.","This describes a homologous series, not isomers.",""]}
{"id":"ch-080","s":"chem","t":"3.3.1","lv":1,"q":"Hydrogen bromide undergoes heterolytic fission. What is formed?","o":["H⁺ and Br⁻","H• and Br•","H⁻ and Br⁺","H₂ and Br₂"],"a":0,"x":"In heterolytic fission both electrons of the bond go to one atom. Bromine is more electronegative, so it takes the pair and forms Br⁻, leaving H⁺. Radicals H• and Br• form in homolytic fission.","w":["","H• and Br• are radicals, formed by homolytic fission, not heterolytic.","Charges swapped: Br is more electronegative so it takes the pair and becomes Br⁻.","Breaking one bond gives two fragments, not H₂ and Br₂."]}
{"id":"ch-081","s":"chem","t":"3.3.1","lv":2,"q":"What is the IUPAC name of CH₃CH₂C(CH₃)=CH₂?","o":["3-methylbut-1-ene","2-methylbut-1-ene","2-methylbut-2-ene","2-ethylprop-1-ene"],"a":1,"x":"The longest chain containing C=C has four carbons: CH₂=C(CH₃)–CH₂–CH₃. Number from the end that gives the double bond the lowest number (1), so the methyl group is on carbon 2.","w":["This puts the methyl on carbon 3, but the structure has it on the carbon of C=C.","","The double bond is at carbon 1 (CH₂=), not between carbons 2 and 3.","Chose a shorter chain; the longest chain containing C=C is four carbons."]}
{"id":"ch-082","s":"chem","t":"3.3.1","lv":2,"q":"In (Z)-1-chloroprop-1-ene, CH₃CH=CHCl, which statement is correct?","o":["The Cl atom and the H atom on the other carbon are on the same side","The CH₃ group and the H atom on the other carbon are on the same side","The Cl atom and the CH₃ group are on the same side of the double bond","The two H atoms attached to the double bond are on opposite sides"],"a":2,"x":"Z means the higher priority groups are on the same side. On each carbon the higher priority group is Cl (over H) and CH₃ (over H). In the Z isomer Cl and CH₃ are together and the two H atoms are together.","w":["In the Z isomer Cl is on the same side as CH₃, so it is opposite the other H.","This is the E isomer: CH₃ and H together means Cl and CH₃ are apart.","","In Z the two H atoms are on the same side, not opposite."]}
{"id":"ch-083","s":"chem","t":"3.3.1","lv":3,"q":"How many alkenes of molecular formula C₅H₁₀ are there, counting E/Z isomers separately and ignoring cyclic isomers?","o":["4","5","7","6"],"a":3,"x":"Pent-1-ene; (E)- and (Z)-pent-2-ene; 2-methylbut-1-ene; 3-methylbut-1-ene; 2-methylbut-2-ene. Only pent-2-ene has two different groups on both carbons of C=C, so only it gives E/Z isomers: 5 structures, 6 isomers.","w":["Too few; there are five structures and pent-2-ene gives two isomers.","Counted the five structures but forgot E/Z pent-2-ene is two isomers.","Overcounted; only pent-2-ene has E/Z isomers, so 7 is too many.",""]}
{"id": "ch-084", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 1, "q": "A burette is read to ±0.05 cm³. A titre of 24.30 cm³ is found from an initial and a final reading. What is the percentage uncertainty in the titre?", "o": ["0.21%", "0.41%", "0.10%", "0.82%"], "a": 1, "x": "Two readings each carry ±0.05 cm³, so the titre is ±0.10 cm³. 0.10 ÷ 24.30 × 100 = 0.41%. The trap is counting only one reading, which gives 0.21%.", "w": ["Used one reading's ±0.05; a titre involves two readings, so ±0.10.", "", "Gave the absolute uncertainty ±0.10 as a percentage; divide by 24.30 and ×100.", "Doubled the uncertainty twice; two readings give ±0.10, not ±0.20."]}
{"id": "ch-085", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 1, "q": "In a titration of an alkali with acid, which piece of apparatus can still be wet with distilled water before use without affecting the result?", "o": ["The burette, before it is filled with acid", "The 25.0 cm³ pipette, before it is filled with alkali", "The conical flask, before the alkali is added to it", "The burette, if the acid is more concentrated than the alkali"], "a": 2, "x": "The pipette fixes the moles of alkali in the flask, so extra water in the flask does not change them. Water left in the burette or pipette dilutes the solution it delivers and changes the result.", "w": ["Water left in the burette dilutes the acid, so the titre changes.", "Water in the pipette dilutes the alkali, so fewer moles are delivered.", "", "Concentration is irrelevant; water in the burette still dilutes the acid."]}
{"id": "ch-086", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 2, "q": "A student makes 250 cm³ of 0.0500 mol dm⁻³ sodium carbonate (Mr = 106.0) as a standard solution. What mass of Na₂CO₃ should be weighed out?", "o": ["1.33 g", "5.30 g", "2.65 g", "0.663 g"], "a": 0, "x": "Moles = 0.0500 × 0.250 = 0.0125 mol. Mass = 0.0125 × 106.0 = 1.325 g, which is 1.33 g. The trap is using 1 dm³ (5.30 g) instead of 250 cm³.", "w": ["", "Used 1 dm³ rather than 250 cm³ (0.250 dm³).", "Used 500 cm³ (0.025 mol); the volume is 250 cm³, giving 0.0125 mol.", "Halved the moles; 0.0500 × 0.250 = 0.0125 mol, not 0.00625."]}
{"id": "ch-087", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 2, "q": "Burning 0.46 g of ethanol (Mr = 46.0) raises the temperature of 100 g of water by 20.0 K. c = 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹. Calculate the enthalpy change of combustion of ethanol.", "o": ["−8.36 kJ mol⁻¹", "+836 kJ mol⁻¹", "−3.85 kJ mol⁻¹", "−836 kJ mol⁻¹"], "a": 3, "x": "q = 100 × 4.18 × 20.0 = 8360 J = 8.36 kJ. Moles ethanol = 0.0100, so ΔH = −836 kJ mol⁻¹ (exothermic). Traps: not dividing by moles, or using the mass of ethanol instead of water.", "w": ["Forgot to divide by 0.0100 mol, so this is just q in kJ, not kJ per mole.", "Sign slip: burning is exothermic, so ΔH must be negative.", "Used the 0.46 g of ethanol as the mass in q = mcΔT instead of the 100 g of water.", ""]}
{"id": "ch-088", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 1, "q": "Marble chips react with excess hydrochloric acid in a flask on a balance, and the mass is recorded over time. Why is loose cotton wool placed in the neck of the flask?", "o": ["To stop CO₂ leaving so the mass stays constant", "To let CO₂ escape but stop acid spray being lost", "To absorb the CO₂ so the mass falls more quickly", "To cool the mixture and slow the reaction"], "a": 1, "x": "The mass loss is the CO₂ escaping, so the gas must be able to leave. Loose cotton wool stops droplets of acid spraying out, which would give a false extra mass loss.", "w": ["The mass drops as CO₂ escapes, so blocking the gas would stop the experiment working.", "", "Cotton wool does not absorb CO₂; the mass falls because the gas leaves.", "Loose cotton wool doesn't cool anything; it only stops acid spray escaping."]}
{"id": "ch-089", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 2, "q": "A gas volume–time graph for a reaction is a curve through the origin. The tangent at t = 0 passes through (0 s, 0 cm³) and (30 s, 18 cm³). What is the initial rate of reaction?", "o": ["0.60 cm³ s⁻¹", "1.7 cm³ s⁻¹", "18 cm³ s⁻¹", "0.060 cm³ s⁻¹"], "a": 0, "x": "Rate = gradient of the tangent = 18 cm³ ÷ 30 s = 0.60 cm³ s⁻¹. Dividing time by volume (1.7) inverts the gradient. Using the volume alone (18) ignores the time.", "w": ["", "Divided time by volume, which inverts the gradient. Rate is volume ÷ time.", "Used the volume alone and ignored the 30 s.", "Decimal slip: 18 ÷ 30 is 0.60, not 0.060."]}
{"id": "ch-090", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 1, "q": "In the test for sulfate ions, dilute hydrochloric acid is added before barium chloride solution. Why?", "o": ["To make barium sulfate more soluble so the precipitate is purer", "To supply the chloride ions needed to form the precipitate", "To remove carbonate ions, which would also give a white precipitate with Ba²⁺", "To speed up the precipitation of barium sulfate"], "a": 2, "x": "Carbonate ions would give white BaCO₃, a false positive. Acid reacts with carbonate and removes it. BaSO₄ is insoluble in acid, so only sulfate gives a precipitate that remains.", "w": ["Acid does not make BaSO₄ more soluble; it is insoluble in acid.", "The chloride comes from the BaCl₂, and Ba²⁺ is what forms the precipitate.", "", "Acid is not there to speed up the precipitation; it removes interfering ions."]}
{"id": "ch-091", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 1, "q": "Acidified silver nitrate gives a cream precipitate. It is insoluble in dilute ammonia solution but dissolves in concentrated ammonia solution. Which halide ion is present?", "o": ["Cl⁻", "I⁻", "F⁻", "Br⁻"], "a": 3, "x": "Bromide gives a cream precipitate that dissolves only in concentrated ammonia. AgCl is white and dissolves in dilute ammonia. AgI is yellow and insoluble in both. AgF is soluble, so no precipitate forms.", "w": ["AgCl is white and dissolves in dilute ammonia, so it does not match.", "AgI is yellow and insoluble even in concentrated ammonia.", "AgF is soluble, so no precipitate forms at all.", ""]}
{"id": "ch-092", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 2, "q": "Propan-1-ol is oxidised to propanoic acid by heating with acidified potassium dichromate(VI). Why is the mixture heated under reflux rather than in an open flask?", "o": ["Reflux raises the boiling point so the reaction is faster", "Vapours condense and return to the flask, so heating continues until complete", "Reflux removes the aldehyde as soon as it forms", "The condenser stops oxygen in the air reacting with the alcohol"], "a": 1, "x": "The condenser returns vaporised alcohol, aldehyde and water to the flask, so the mixture can be heated for a long time without loss. Removing the aldehyde as it forms is distillation, not reflux.", "w": ["Reflux does not raise the boiling point; the mixture still boils at the same temperature.", "", "Removing the aldehyde as it forms is distillation, not reflux.", "The condenser returns vapours to the flask; it is not there to keep air out."]}
{"id": "ch-093", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 3, "q": "12.5 g of cyclohexanol (C₆H₁₂O, Mr = 100.0) is dehydrated to cyclohexene (C₆H₁₀, Mr = 82.0). After distillation, 5.74 g of cyclohexene is obtained. What is the percentage yield?", "o": ["45.9%", "37.7%", "56.0%", "44.0%"], "a": 2, "x": "Moles alcohol = 0.125, and the ratio is 1:1. Theoretical mass = 0.125 × 82.0 = 10.25 g. Yield = 5.74 ÷ 10.25 = 56.0%. Dividing by the starting mass (45.9%) ignores the change in Mr.", "w": ["Divided by the starting mass, ignoring that cyclohexene has a different Mr.", "Applied the Mr ratio the wrong way: 45.9% × 82/100 instead of 45.9% × 100/82.", "", "Subtracted 56.0% from 100% to get the percentage lost, not the yield."]}
{"id": "ch-094", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 2, "q": "Four colourless liquids are each warmed with acidified potassium dichromate(VI). Which one stays orange?", "o": ["2-methylpropan-2-ol", "butan-2-ol", "butan-1-ol", "butanal"], "a": 0, "x": "Tertiary alcohols cannot be oxidised, so 2-methylpropan-2-ol stays orange. Primary alcohols, secondary alcohols and aldehydes are all oxidised, turning the dichromate from orange to green.", "w": ["", "Butan-2-ol is secondary, so it is oxidised to a ketone and turns green.", "Butan-1-ol is primary, so it is oxidised and turns green.", "Aldehydes are easily oxidised to acids, so it turns green."]}
{"id": "ch-095", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 1, "q": "A student calculates n = 1.85 g ÷ 74.1 g mol⁻¹ = 0.0249662… mol. Both values are given to 3 significant figures. How should the answer be written?", "o": ["0.025 mol", "0.02497 mol", "0.0249662 mol", "0.0250 mol"], "a": 3, "x": "The answer should match the data, which is 3 significant figures: 0.0250 mol. The trailing zero is significant. 0.025 has only 2 s.f., and the other options keep too many digits from the calculator.", "w": ["Rounded to 2 s.f., which loses precision the data justify.", "Gives 4 s.f., which is more precision than the data support.", "Copied the calculator display, far too many s.f. for 3 s.f. data.", ""]}
{"id": "ch-096", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 2, "q": "A balance reads to ±0.005 g. A weighing bottle is 5.62 g before and 4.37 g after solid is tipped out. What is the percentage uncertainty in the 1.25 g transferred?", "o": ["0.40%", "0.089%", "0.80%", "0.18%"], "a": 2, "x": "Mass by difference uses two readings, so the uncertainty is ±0.010 g. 0.010 ÷ 1.25 × 100 = 0.80%. Using one reading gives 0.40%, and dividing by the bottle mass is wrong.", "w": ["Used one reading's ±0.005 g; mass by difference involves two readings.", "Divided ±0.005 g by the bottle's 5.62 g instead of the 1.25 g transferred.", "", "Used ±0.010 g correctly but divided by the 5.62 g starting mass, not 1.25 g."]}
{"id": "ch-097", "s": "chem", "t": "8.rp", "lv": 3, "q": "25.0 cm³ portions of NaOH(aq) are titrated with 0.0500 mol dm⁻³ H₂SO₄. Titres: 23.15 (rough), 22.40, 22.35, 22.45 cm³. What is the concentration of the NaOH?", "o": ["0.0896 mol dm⁻³", "0.0448 mol dm⁻³", "0.0926 mol dm⁻³", "0.0558 mol dm⁻³"], "a": 0, "x": "Mean of concordant titres = 22.40 cm³. Moles H₂SO₄ = 1.12 × 10⁻³, so NaOH = 2.24 × 10⁻³ (2:1). Divide by 0.0250 dm³ to get 0.0896. Using the rough titre gives 0.0926, and ignoring the 2:1 ratio gives 0.0448.", "w": ["", "Ignored the 2:1 NaOH:H₂SO₄ ratio, giving half the correct value.", "Used only the rough titre (23.15), which should be ignored.", "Swapped the volumes (0.0500 × 25.0 ÷ 22.40) and also ignored the 2:1 ratio."]}
{"id":"ch-098","s":"chem","t":"3.1.4","lv":2,"q":"Excess zinc powder is added to 50.0 cm³ of 0.200 mol dm⁻³ CuSO₄(aq). The temperature rises by 10.0 °C. Take c = 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹ and density 1.00 g cm⁻³. What is ΔH for Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu?","o":["+209 kJ mol⁻¹","−209 kJ mol⁻¹","−2.09 kJ mol⁻¹","−20.9 kJ mol⁻¹"],"a":1,"x":"q = mcΔT = 50.0 × 4.18 × 10.0 = 2090 J = 2.09 kJ. Zinc is in excess, so Cu²⁺ is limiting: n = 0.0500 × 0.200 = 0.0100 mol. ΔH = −2.09 ÷ 0.0100 = −209 kJ mol⁻¹. It is negative because the temperature rises.","w":["Sign slip: the temperature rises, so the reaction is exothermic and ΔH must be negative.","","Forgot to divide by moles; 2.09 kJ is just the heat released to the solution.","Used 0.100 mol instead of 0.0100 mol, a decimal-place slip when converting 50.0 cm³ to dm³."]}
{"id":"ch-099","s":"chem","t":"3.1.5","lv":1,"q":"The pressure of a gaseous reaction mixture is increased at constant temperature. Which statement best explains why the rate increases?","o":["A greater proportion of molecules have energy ≥ the activation energy","Collisions are more frequent because there are more molecules in a given volume","The activation energy of the reaction is lowered by the higher pressure","Each collision has more energy because the molecules are closer together"],"a":1,"x":"Higher pressure packs more molecules into each unit volume, so collisions are more frequent. Temperature is unchanged, so the energy distribution and the fraction with E ≥ Ea stay the same.","w":["The energy distribution depends on temperature, not pressure, so the proportion with E ≥ Ea is unchanged.","","Only a catalyst provides a route with lower Ea; pressure does not change it.","Collision energy depends on temperature; being closer together raises collision frequency, not energy."]}
{"id":"ch-100","s":"chem","t":"3.1.5","lv":2,"q":"In a disappearing-cross experiment, the time for the cross to vanish falls from 120 s to 45 s when the temperature is raised. Taking rate ∝ 1/time, by what factor has the rate increased?","o":["0.375","75","165","2.7"],"a":3,"x":"Rate ∝ 1/t, so the rate ratio is 120 ÷ 45 ≈ 2.7. The trap is treating time as if it were the rate, or subtracting or adding the times.","w":["This is 45 ÷ 120, the inverted ratio, which treats the time as proportional to rate.","This subtracts the two times (120 − 45), but the rate ratio comes from dividing the reciprocals.","This adds the two times, which has no meaning for comparing rates.",""]}
{"id":"ch-101","s":"chem","t":"3.1.5","lv":3,"q":"A catalyst is added to a gaseous reaction at constant temperature. Which row correctly describes the Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution and the activation energy marker, Ea?","o":["Curve unchanged; Ea marker moves to lower energy; larger proportion has E ≥ Ea","Curve shifts right and flattens; Ea marker unchanged; larger proportion has E ≥ Ea","Curve unchanged; Ea marker unchanged; collisions are simply more frequent","Curve peak moves left; Ea marker moves to lower energy; total area increases"],"a":0,"x":"A catalyst does not change the molecular energies, so the curve stays put. It lowers Ea, so the marker moves left and a larger area lies to its right. The trap is confusing this with heating.","w":["","That describes raising the temperature; a catalyst does not change the energies of the molecules.","A catalyst lowers Ea, so the marker moves, and the proportion with E ≥ Ea rises; collision frequency is not the cause.","The curve depends on temperature and is unchanged, and the area is the fixed total number of molecules."]}
{"id":"ch-102","s":"chem","t":"3.1.6","lv":2,"q":"At a certain temperature, Kc for N₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g) is 0.0050 mol dm⁻³. What is Kc for 2NO₂(g) ⇌ N₂O₄(g) at the same temperature?","o":["100 dm³ mol⁻¹","200 dm³ mol⁻¹","4.0 × 10⁴ dm⁶ mol⁻²","0.0050 mol dm⁻³"],"a":1,"x":"Reversing the equation inverts the expression: Kc = [N₂O₄] ÷ [NO₂]² = 1 ÷ 0.0050 = 200. Units are the reciprocal, dm³ mol⁻¹. The trap is keeping the same value or altering it by the coefficients.","w":["Divided the reciprocal by 2 for the coefficient; the 2 is already the power in the expression.","","Squared the reciprocal; the stoichiometric powers are already in the expression, so only a single inversion is needed.","Assumed Kc is unchanged when the equation is reversed; it becomes the reciprocal, with reciprocal units."]}
{"id":"ch-103","s":"chem","t":"3.1.6","lv":3,"q":"H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g) has Kc = 49 at a given temperature. 1.00 mol H₂ and 1.00 mol I₂ are allowed to reach equilibrium in a 5.0 dm³ vessel. How many moles of HI are present?","o":["0.78 mol","0.875 mol","1.75 mol","1.56 mol"],"a":3,"x":"Volume cancels as there are equal gas moles each side. (2x)² ÷ (1−x)² = 49, so 2x ÷ (1−x) = 7. Then x = 0.778 and HI = 2x = 1.56 mol. The trap is giving x instead of 2x.","w":["This is x, the moles of H₂ reacted; HI formed is 2x because the ratio is 1 : 2.","Solved x ÷ (1−x) = 7, forgetting the 2 in 2x, so x is wrong; this is that x value.","Solved x ÷ (1−x) = 7 (forgetting the 2 in 2x) and then doubled; the algebra is wrong from the start.",""]}
{"id":"ch-104","s":"chem","t":"3.1.7","lv":2,"q":"Chlorine reacts with cold aqueous alkali: Cl₂ + 2OH⁻ → Cl⁻ + ClO⁻ + H₂O. Which statement about the oxidation state of chlorine is correct?","o":["It changes from 0 to −1 only, so chlorine is reduced only","It changes from 0 to −1 and from 0 to +1, so chlorine is both reduced and oxidised","It changes from 0 to +1 only, because ClO⁻ contains oxygen, so chlorine is oxidised only","It changes from 0 to −1 and from 0 to +2, so chlorine is both reduced and oxidised"],"a":1,"x":"In Cl₂ chlorine is 0, in Cl⁻ it is −1 (reduction) and in ClO⁻ it is +1, since O is −2 and the ion is 1−. One element both oxidised and reduced is disproportionation.","w":["This ignores ClO⁻, where chlorine rises to +1, so it is also oxidised.","","Ignores Cl⁻, where chlorine falls to −1; the same element is reduced as well as oxidised.","In ClO⁻, O is −2 and the charge is 1−, so Cl is +1, not +2."]}
{"id":"ch-105","s":"chem","t":"3.1.7","lv":3,"q":"MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O and SO₃²⁻ + H₂O → SO₄²⁻ + 2H⁺ + 2e⁻. Which is the correct overall ionic equation for MnO₄⁻ oxidising SO₃²⁻ in acid?","o":["MnO₄⁻ + SO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → Mn²⁺ + SO₄²⁻ + H₂O","2MnO₄⁻ + 5SO₃²⁻ + 16H⁺ → 2Mn²⁺ + 5SO₄²⁻ + 8H₂O","5MnO₄⁻ + 2SO₃²⁻ + 6H⁺ → 5Mn²⁺ + 2SO₄²⁻ + 3H₂O","2MnO₄⁻ + 5SO₃²⁻ + 6H⁺ → 2Mn²⁺ + 5SO₄²⁻ + 3H₂O"],"a":3,"x":"Make electrons equal at 10: 2 × the MnO₄⁻ half-equation plus 5 × the SO₃²⁻ half-equation. Then cancel 16H⁺ − 10H⁺ = 6H⁺ and 8H₂O − 5H₂O = 3H₂O. Charge: −6 on each side.","w":["Electrons are not balanced: 5 are gained but only 2 lost, and the charges differ (−1 left, 0 right).","Keeps 16H⁺ and 8H₂O without cancelling the sulfite half-equation's 5H₂O and 10H⁺, so O and charge do not balance.","Ratio inverted: MnO₄⁻ takes 5 electrons and SO₃²⁻ gives 2, so 2 MnO₄⁻ react with 5 SO₃²⁻.",""]}
{"id":"ch-106","s":"chem","t":"3.1.8","lv":1,"q":"Which statement defines the lattice enthalpy of formation of an ionic compound?","o":["Enthalpy change when 1 mol of the solid forms from its elements in their standard states","Enthalpy change when 1 mol of the solid ionic compound forms from its gaseous ions","Enthalpy change when 1 mol of the solid ionic lattice is broken into its gaseous ions","Enthalpy change when 1 mol of gaseous ions dissolves in water to form aqueous ions"],"a":1,"x":"Lattice enthalpy of formation is the enthalpy change when one mole of solid ionic compound forms from its gaseous ions, so it is exothermic. Do not confuse it with enthalpy of formation or with the dissociation (reverse) definition.","w":["This is the standard enthalpy of formation, which starts from elements, not gaseous ions.","","This is lattice dissociation enthalpy, the reverse process and endothermic, not formation.","This is enthalpy of hydration of gaseous ions, not lattice enthalpy."]}
{"id":"ch-107","s":"chem","t":"3.1.8","lv":2,"q":"Calculate the lattice enthalpy of formation of NaCl (kJ mol⁻¹) using: ΔHf(NaCl) −411; ΔHat(Na) +107; ΔHat(Cl) +122; 1st IE(Na) +496; 1st EA(Cl) −349.","o":["−787","−909","−665","−1485"],"a":0,"x":"Apply Hess's law: −411 = +107 + 122 + 496 − 349 + ΔHlatt. The steps sum to +376, so ΔHlatt = −411 − 376 = −787 kJ mol⁻¹. The trap is using the wrong chlorine step or the wrong sign for electron affinity.","w":["","This uses 244, the Cl₂ bond enthalpy, instead of the 122 needed for one mole of Cl atoms.","This leaves out the atomisation of chlorine, so a step in the cycle is missing.","This treats the electron affinity as +349 instead of −349, a sign slip."]}
{"id":"ch-108","s":"chem","t":"3.1.8","lv":2,"q":"CaCO₃(s) → CaO(s) + CO₂(g). S° values (J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹): CaCO₃ 92.9, CaO 39.7, CO₂ 213.6. What is ΔS° for the reaction?","o":["−160.4 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹","+120.7 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹","+346.2 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹","+160.4 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹"],"a":3,"x":"ΔS = ΣS(products) − ΣS(reactants) = (39.7 + 213.6) − 92.9 = +160.4 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. It is positive because a gas forms from solids. Subtract reactants from products, not the other way round.","w":["This is reactants minus products, the wrong way round; a gas is formed so entropy must increase.","This leaves out the CaO entropy, so only one of the two products is counted.","This adds the reactant entropy to the products instead of subtracting it.",""]}
{"id":"ch-109","s":"chem","t":"3.1.8","lv":2,"q":"A decomposition has ΔH = +178 kJ mol⁻¹ and ΔS = +160 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. Assuming both are constant, what is the minimum temperature at which it becomes feasible?","o":["1.11 K","840 K","1113 K","1.11 × 10⁶ K"],"a":2,"x":"Feasible when ΔG ≤ 0, so T ≥ ΔH/ΔS. Convert ΔH to J: 178 000 ÷ 160 = 1113 K. The traps are mixing kJ and J, and subtracting 273 when the answer is wanted in kelvin.","w":["This divides 178 by 160 without converting kJ to J, so the units are inconsistent.","This subtracts 273 as if converting to °C, but the question asks for kelvin.","","This converts ΔS to kJ but also ΔH to J, so the units are mismatched by 10⁶."]}
{"id":"ch-110","s":"chem","t":"3.1.8","lv":2,"q":"Which compound is most likely to have an experimental lattice enthalpy much more exothermic than the value from the perfect ionic model?","o":["NaCl","MgO","LiF","AgI"],"a":3,"x":"The perfect ionic model assumes purely ionic bonding with spherical ions. Ag⁺ is polarising and I⁻ is large and easily polarised, so AgI has covalent character, making the experimental value more exothermic than theory.","w":["Na⁺ and Cl⁻ are close to the perfect ionic model, so theory and experiment agree well.","Highly charged ions do not by themselves mean covalent character; MgO agrees closely with the ionic model.","Li⁺ and F⁻ are both small and hard to polarise, so LiF is close to purely ionic.",""]}
{"id":"ch-111","s":"chem","t":"3.1.8","lv":3,"q":"For CaCl₂: lattice enthalpy of formation −2258 kJ mol⁻¹; ΔHhyd Ca²⁺ −1650 kJ mol⁻¹; ΔHhyd Cl⁻ −364 kJ mol⁻¹. Calculate the enthalpy of solution of CaCl₂.","o":["−120 kJ mol⁻¹","+244 kJ mol⁻¹","+120 kJ mol⁻¹","−4636 kJ mol⁻¹"],"a":0,"x":"Dissolving first breaks the lattice, so use +2258, then add hydration of each ion, including two Cl⁻: +2258 − 1650 − 2(364) = −120 kJ mol⁻¹. The traps are not reversing the lattice sign and counting only one chloride.","w":["","This counts only one Cl⁻ hydration (+2258 − 1650 − 364), but the formula has two chloride ions.","This reverses the sign of every term, so the exothermic result is shown as endothermic.","This uses the lattice formation value without reversing it, but dissolving breaks the lattice."]}
{"id":"ch-112","s":"chem","t":"3.1.9","lv":2,"q":"A reaction has the rate equation: rate = k[X][Y]². What are the overall order and the units of the rate constant k?","o":["Second order; mol⁻¹ dm³ s⁻¹","Third order; mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹","Third order; mol⁻² dm⁶ s⁻¹","Third order; mol⁻¹ dm³ s⁻¹"],"a":2,"x":"The orders add: 1 + 2 = 3. Rearranging, k = rate/([X][Y]²), so the units are mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹ ÷ mol³ dm⁻⁹ = mol⁻² dm⁶ s⁻¹. The trap is using units for the wrong overall order.","w":["Counted the two species instead of adding the orders 1 + 2; these are second-order units.","These are the units of rate, not of k; k must cancel the concentration terms.","","Correct order but the units are those for second order; third order needs mol⁻² dm⁶ s⁻¹."]}
{"id":"ch-113","s":"chem","t":"3.1.9","lv":2,"q":"Initial rates for A + B → products: (0.10, 0.10 mol dm⁻³) gives 2.0×10⁻³; (0.20, 0.10) gives 4.0×10⁻³; (0.20, 0.30) gives 3.6×10⁻² mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹. What is k?","o":["2.0 mol⁻² dm⁶ s⁻¹","0.20 mol⁻¹ dm³ s⁻¹","0.60 mol⁻¹ dm³ s⁻¹","20 mol⁻² dm⁶ s⁻¹"],"a":0,"x":"Doubling [A] doubles the rate, so first order in A. Tripling [B] multiplies the rate by 9, so second order in B. Rate = k[A][B]², so k = 2.0×10⁻³ ÷ (0.10 × 0.10²) = 2.0 mol⁻² dm⁶ s⁻¹.","w":["","Assumed first order in B (rate = k[A][B]); tripling [B] gave ×9, so B is second order.","Used experiment 3 with first order in B; the rate rose ×9 when [B] rose ×3, so B is second order.","Right rate equation but a powers-of-ten slip: 0.10 × 0.10² = 1.0×10⁻³, giving k = 2.0, not 20."]}
{"id":"ch-114","s":"chem","t":"3.1.9","lv":2,"q":"A plot of ln k against 1/T for a reaction is a straight line with gradient −6.00×10³ K. Using R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹, what is the activation energy?","o":["0.722 kJ mol⁻¹","49 900 kJ mol⁻¹","−49.9 kJ mol⁻¹","49.9 kJ mol⁻¹"],"a":3,"x":"$\\ln k = -\\dfrac{E_a}{RT} + \\ln A$, so gradient = −Eₐ/R. Eₐ = 6000 × 8.31 = 49 860 J mol⁻¹ ≈ 49.9 kJ mol⁻¹. Trap: dividing by R, or forgetting to convert J to kJ.","w":["Divided the gradient by R instead of multiplying, then converted to kJ; Eₐ = −gradient × R.","Correct value in J mol⁻¹ but not converted to kJ; the unit slip gives a value 1000 times too large.","Kept the gradient's negative sign; the gradient is −Eₐ/R, so Eₐ itself is positive.",""]}
{"id":"ch-115","s":"chem","t":"3.1.9","lv":2,"q":"2NO₂ + F₂ → 2NO₂F has the rate equation: rate = k[NO₂][F₂]. Which description of the rate-determining step is consistent with this?","o":["Two NO₂ molecules react together","One NO₂ molecule reacts with one F₂ molecule","Two NO₂ molecules react with one F₂ molecule","One F₂ molecule splits into two F atoms alone"],"a":1,"x":"The rate equation shows which species are in the rate-determining step: one NO₂ and one F₂. The stoichiometric equation (2NO₂) does not give the orders. A termolecular step is also very unlikely.","w":["This would give rate = k[NO₂]², with no F₂ term, which does not match the rate equation.","","Copied the coefficients from the overall equation; orders come from experiment, not stoichiometry, and this would give [NO₂]²[F₂].","This would give rate = k[F₂] only, with no NO₂ in the rate equation."]}
{"id":"ch-116","s":"chem","t":"3.1.9","lv":2,"q":"A first-order reaction is followed continuously with a colorimeter. [A] falls from 0.80 mol dm⁻³ to 0.10 mol dm⁻³ in 36 minutes. What is the half-life?","o":["4.5 min","9.0 min","12 min","18 min"],"a":2,"x":"For a first-order reaction the half-life is constant. 0.80 → 0.40 → 0.20 → 0.10 is three half-lives, so t½ = 36 ÷ 3 = 12 min. The trap is miscounting the number of halvings.","w":["Divided 36 by 8, the overall fold-change in concentration, instead of the number of halvings (3).","Counted four halvings; 0.80 to 0.10 is only three (0.40, 0.20, 0.10).","","Counted only two half-lives; 0.80 to 0.20 is two, but 0.10 needs a third."]}
{"id":"ch-117","s":"chem","t":"3.1.9","lv":3,"q":"A reaction has Eₐ = 50.0 kJ mol⁻¹. By what factor does k change when the temperature rises from 300 K to 310 K? (R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","o":["0.52","0.65","1.0","1.9"],"a":3,"x":"$\\ln(k_2/k_1) = \\dfrac{E_a}{R}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{T_1}-\\dfrac{1}{T_2}\\right) = \\dfrac{50000}{8.31}\\times 1.075\\times10^{-4} = 0.647$. Then k₂/k₁ = e^0.647 ≈ 1.9. Traps: not exponentiating, or using kJ rather than J.","w":["Inverted the ratio (k₁/k₂), or reversed the temperature terms; k rises with temperature so the factor is above 1.","Stopped at ln(k₂/k₁) = 0.647 and forgot to take the exponential to find the ratio.","Used Eₐ in kJ with R in J, so the ln term is 1000 times too small and k looks unchanged.",""]}
{"id":"ch-118","s":"chem","t":"3.1.10","lv":1,"q":"Which is the correct expression for Kp for the equilibrium N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)?","o":["$K_p = \\dfrac{p(\\mathrm{NH_3})}{p(\\mathrm{N_2})\\,p(\\mathrm{H_2})}$","$K_p = \\dfrac{p(\\mathrm{N_2})\\,p(\\mathrm{H_2})^3}{p(\\mathrm{NH_3})^2}$","$K_p = \\dfrac{p(\\mathrm{NH_3})^2}{p(\\mathrm{N_2})\\,p(\\mathrm{H_2})^3}$","$K_p = \\dfrac{2\\,p(\\mathrm{NH_3})}{p(\\mathrm{N_2})\\times 3\\,p(\\mathrm{H_2})}$"],"a":2,"x":"Kp is products over reactants, with each partial pressure raised to the power of its coefficient. This gives p(NH₃)² over p(N₂)p(H₂)³. The traps are inverting the fraction or ignoring the powers.","w":["Ignores the coefficients; each partial pressure must be raised to the power of its balancing number.","Inverted: Kp has products on top and reactants on the bottom.","","Uses coefficients as multipliers instead of powers; 2NH₃ means p(NH₃)², not 2p(NH₃)."]}
{"id":"ch-119","s":"chem","t":"3.1.10","lv":1,"q":"For N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), ΔH is negative. The total pressure is increased at constant temperature. What happens to Kp and to the equilibrium yield of NH₃?","o":["Kp is unchanged and the yield of NH₃ increases","Kp increases and the yield of NH₃ increases","Kp is unchanged and the yield of NH₃ decreases","Kp decreases and the yield of NH₃ increases"],"a":0,"x":"Only temperature changes Kp. Higher pressure shifts the position to the side with fewer gas moles (4 → 2), so more NH₃ forms, but the value of Kp stays the same.","w":["","Kp does not change with pressure; only the position of equilibrium shifts, so Kp cannot increase.","Higher pressure favours the side with fewer gas moles, which is NH₃, so the yield rises.","Kp is unaffected by pressure; a fall in Kp would need a temperature change."]}
{"id":"ch-120","s":"chem","t":"3.1.10","lv":2,"q":"PCl₅(g) ⇌ PCl₃(g) + Cl₂(g). At equilibrium the partial pressures are PCl₅ 15 kPa, PCl₃ 30 kPa, Cl₂ 30 kPa. What is Kp at this temperature?","o":["0.017 kPa⁻¹","60 kPa","120 kPa","2.0 kPa"],"a":1,"x":"Kp = p(PCl₃)p(Cl₂)/p(PCl₅) = (30 × 30)/15 = 60. There are two gas moles on top and one below, so the unit is kPa. Inverting the fraction is the common trap.","w":["Inverted the expression, putting reactants over products; Kp has products on top.","","Wrongly multiplied by 2; the coefficients of 1 give no extra factor.","Left out p(Cl₂), using only 30/15; both products appear in the numerator."]}
{"id":"ch-121","s":"chem","t":"3.1.10","lv":2,"q":"N₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g). An equilibrium mixture at total pressure 200 kPa contains 0.40 mol N₂O₄ and 1.20 mol NO₂. What is Kp?","o":["2.25 kPa","3.0 kPa","900 kPa","450 kPa"],"a":3,"x":"Mole fractions are 0.25 and 0.75, so partial pressures are 50 kPa and 150 kPa. Kp = 150²/50 = 450 kPa. Traps: forgetting to square p(NO₂), or using mole fractions instead of pressures.","w":["Used mole fractions 0.75²/0.25 instead of partial pressures; multiply by total pressure first.","Did not square p(NO₂): 150/50; the coefficient 2 means a power of 2.","Multiplied by the coefficient 2 as well as squaring; coefficients appear only as powers.",""]}
{"id":"ch-122","s":"chem","t":"3.1.10","lv":2,"q":"2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g), ΔH = −196 kJ mol⁻¹. The temperature of the equilibrium mixture is raised. Which statement is correct?","o":["Kp increases and the equilibrium moves right","Kp is unchanged and the equilibrium moves left","Kp decreases and the equilibrium moves left","Kp decreases and the equilibrium moves right"],"a":2,"x":"The forward reaction is exothermic, so raising temperature favours the endothermic reverse reaction. The position moves left and Kp falls, since temperature is the only factor that changes Kp.","w":["Treats the forward reaction as endothermic; it is exothermic, so heating favours the reverse reaction.","Temperature is the one factor that does change Kp; a shift in position here means Kp changes.","","Kp falling means less product relative to reactant, so the position must move left, not right."]}
{"id":"ch-123","s":"chem","t":"3.1.10","lv":3,"q":"Pure N₂O₄(g) at 100 kPa partly dissociates: N₂O₄ ⇌ 2NO₂. At equilibrium at constant volume and temperature, p(NO₂) = 40 kPa. What is Kp?","o":["20 kPa","27 kPa","16 kPa","0.50 kPa"],"a":0,"x":"NO₂ at 40 kPa means 20 kPa of N₂O₄ reacted, leaving 80 kPa. Kp = 40²/80 = 20 kPa. Traps: subtracting 40 rather than 20, using the initial 100 kPa, or not squaring.","w":["","Subtracted 40 from 100, ignoring that one N₂O₄ gives two NO₂ so only 20 reacted.","Used the initial 100 kPa for N₂O₄ instead of the equilibrium value of 80 kPa.","Did not square p(NO₂): 40/80; the coefficient 2 requires a squared term."]}
{"id":"ch-124","s":"chem","t":"3.1.11","lv":1,"q":"Which set of conditions describes the standard hydrogen electrode, against which all standard electrode potentials are measured?","o":["H₂ at 100 kPa, 298 K, 0.10 mol dm⁻³ H⁺(aq), platinum electrode","H₂ at 100 kPa, 298 K, 1.00 mol dm⁻³ H⁺(aq), platinum electrode","H₂ at 100 kPa, 273 K, 1.00 mol dm⁻³ H⁺(aq), platinum electrode","H₂ at 100 kPa, 298 K, 1.00 mol dm⁻³ H⁺(aq), zinc electrode"],"a":1,"x":"The standard hydrogen electrode uses H₂ at 100 kPa bubbling over an inert platinum electrode in 1.00 mol dm⁻³ H⁺(aq) at 298 K. Its potential is defined as exactly 0.00 V.","w":["The H⁺ concentration must be 1.00 mol dm⁻³; 0.10 mol dm⁻³ is not a standard condition.","","Standard conditions use 298 K; 273 K (0 °C) is a common mix-up.","Zinc is reactive and would dissolve in acid; the electrode must be inert platinum."]}
{"id":"ch-125","s":"chem","t":"3.1.11","lv":1,"q":"A standard cell joins a Zn²⁺(aq)|Zn half-cell (E° = −0.76 V) to an Ag⁺(aq)|Ag half-cell (E° = +0.80 V). What is the standard EMF of the cell?","o":["+1.56 V","+0.04 V","+3.12 V","−1.56 V"],"a":0,"x":"EMF = E°(positive electrode) − E°(negative electrode) = 0.80 − (−0.76) = +1.56 V. Subtracting a negative value means adding. Electrode potentials are never multiplied by the number of electrons.","w":["","Subtracted the magnitudes, treating −0.76 as +0.76 and ignoring the negative sign.","Doubled the value to balance two electrons; E° is not scaled by stoichiometry.","Subtracted the wrong way round; a working cell has a positive EMF, with the more positive electrode as the positive terminal."]}
{"id":"ch-126","s":"chem","t":"3.1.11","lv":2,"q":"A cell links Fe²⁺(aq)|Fe (E° = −0.44 V) with Ni²⁺(aq)|Ni (E° = −0.25 V) under standard conditions. Which statement about the cell is correct?","o":["The Fe electrode is positive; EMF is 0.19 V; electrons flow from Ni to Fe","The Ni electrode is positive; EMF is 0.69 V; electrons flow from Fe to Ni","The Ni electrode is positive; EMF is 0.19 V; electrons flow from Fe to Ni","The Ni electrode is positive; EMF is 0.19 V; electrons flow from Ni to Fe"],"a":2,"x":"Ni has the more positive E°, so it is the positive terminal. EMF = −0.25 − (−0.44) = 0.19 V. Fe is oxidised and releases electrons, which flow through the wire from Fe (negative) to Ni (positive).","w":["Took the more negative E° as the positive terminal; the more positive electrode is positive, so polarity and flow are both reversed.","Added the magnitudes 0.44 and 0.25 instead of subtracting; both potentials are negative, so the difference is 0.19 V.","","Electrons flow from the negative electrode to the positive one, so from Fe to Ni, not Ni to Fe."]}
{"id":"ch-127","s":"chem","t":"3.1.11","lv":2,"q":"E°: Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺ +0.77 V; I₂/I⁻ +0.54 V; Br₂/Br⁻ +1.07 V; Cl₂/Cl⁻ +1.36 V. Which reaction is feasible under standard conditions?","o":["Fe³⁺ oxidising Br⁻ to Br₂","I₂ oxidising Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺","Br₂ oxidising Cl⁻ to Cl₂","Fe³⁺ oxidising I⁻ to I₂"],"a":3,"x":"A reaction is feasible if E°cell is positive: oxidising agent E° minus reducing agent E°. Fe³⁺ with I⁻ gives 0.77 − 0.54 = +0.23 V. The other three give −0.30 V, −0.23 V and −0.29 V.","w":["E°cell = 0.77 − 1.07 = −0.30 V; Fe³⁺ is too weak an oxidising agent to oxidise Br⁻.","E°cell = 0.54 − 0.77 = −0.23 V; the oxidising agent is the species with the more positive E°, and I₂ is the weaker.","E°cell = 1.07 − 1.36 = −0.29 V; chlorine oxidises bromide, not the reverse.",""]}
{"id":"ch-128","s":"chem","t":"3.1.11","lv":3,"q":"A standard Zn|Zn²⁺ || Cu²⁺|Cu cell has EMF 1.10 V. The Cu²⁺(aq) solution is then diluted tenfold with water. What happens to the EMF, and why?","o":["Falls; equilibrium Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ ⇌ Cu shifts left, so Cu electrode less positive","Rises; equilibrium Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ ⇌ Cu shifts left, so Cu electrode more positive","Stays at 1.10 V; electrode potentials do not depend on concentration","Rises; equilibrium Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ ⇌ Cu shifts right, so Cu electrode more positive"],"a":0,"x":"Diluting Cu²⁺ moves the equilibrium Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ ⇌ Cu to the left (Le Chatelier). The copper electrode becomes less positive, so the EMF falls below 1.10 V. The standard value 1.10 V only applies at 1.00 mol dm⁻³.","w":["","A leftward shift makes the electrode less positive, not more, so the EMF cannot rise by this reasoning.","E° is fixed, but the actual electrode potential changes with concentration, so non-standard EMF differs from 1.10 V.","Less Cu²⁺ cannot shift the equilibrium right; removing a reactant shifts it left, which lowers the potential."]}
{"id":"ch-129","s":"chem","t":"3.1.11","lv":1,"q":"A hydrogen–oxygen fuel cell has an acidic electrolyte. Which half-equation takes place at the positive electrode?","o":["H₂ → 2H⁺ + 2e⁻","O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ → 2H₂O","O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻","2H₂O → O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻"],"a":1,"x":"At the positive electrode oxygen is reduced. In acid it combines with H⁺ and electrons to make water: O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ → 2H₂O. The OH⁻ version is for alkaline electrolytes, and oxidation of hydrogen is at the negative electrode.","w":["This is the oxidation of hydrogen, which occurs at the negative electrode, not the positive one.","","This is the oxygen half-equation for an alkaline electrolyte; in acid, H⁺ is used instead.","This is the reverse reaction: electrons are lost, but reduction occurs at the positive electrode of a cell."]}
{"id":"ch-130","s":"chem","t":"3.1.12","lv":1,"q":"What is the pH of 0.020 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid at 298 K?","o":["2.00","1.70","1.40","12.30"],"a":1,"x":"HCl is a strong monobasic acid, so it is fully dissociated and [H⁺] = 0.020 mol dm⁻³. pH = −log(0.020) = 1.70. Rounding the log to 2 is a common slip.","w":["Rounding the log of 0.020 to −2 treats the concentration as 0.010 mol dm⁻³; pH = −log(0.020) = 1.70.","","This is −log(0.040), wrongly doubling [H⁺] as if HCl were dibasic; it releases one H⁺ per molecule.","This is 14 − 1.70, the pH of an alkali; the acid itself has a low pH."]}
{"id":"ch-131","s":"chem","t":"3.1.12","lv":2,"q":"What is the pH of 0.0250 mol dm⁻³ barium hydroxide, Ba(OH)₂, at 298 K, where Kw = 1.00 × 10⁻¹⁴ mol² dm⁻⁶?","o":["12.40","1.30","1.60","12.70"],"a":3,"x":"Ba(OH)₂ gives two OH⁻ per formula unit, so [OH⁻] = 0.0500 mol dm⁻³. [H⁺] = Kw ÷ [OH⁻] = 2.00 × 10⁻¹³, so pH = 12.70. Forgetting the factor of 2 gives 12.40.","w":["This uses [OH⁻] = 0.0250 and ignores that each Ba(OH)₂ releases two OH⁻ ions.","This is the pOH, −log(0.0500); the pH is found from [H⁺] = Kw ÷ [OH⁻], or 14 − pOH.","This is −log(0.0250), applying the pH formula directly to the hydroxide concentration of an alkali.",""]}
{"id":"ch-132","s":"chem","t":"3.1.12","lv":2,"q":"A 0.100 mol dm⁻³ solution of a weak monobasic acid HA has Ka = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³. Assuming [H⁺] = [A⁻] and negligible dissociation, what is its pH?","o":["2.87","4.74","5.74","1.00"],"a":0,"x":"Ka = [H⁺]² ÷ [HA], so [H⁺] = √(1.8 × 10⁻⁵ × 0.100) = 1.34 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³. pH = 2.87. The trap is leaving out the square root.","w":["","This is the pKa of the acid, not the pH of its solution.","This is −log(1.8 × 10⁻⁶), which forgets to take the square root of Ka × [HA].","This treats HA as strong and fully dissociated, but a weak acid only partly dissociates."]}
{"id":"ch-133","s":"chem","t":"3.1.12","lv":2,"q":"A buffer contains 0.20 mol dm⁻³ ethanoic acid and 0.10 mol dm⁻³ sodium ethanoate. Ka of ethanoic acid = 1.74 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³. What is its pH?","o":["2.73","4.46","4.76","5.06"],"a":1,"x":"[H⁺] = Ka × [acid] ÷ [salt] = 1.74 × 10⁻⁵ × 2 = 3.48 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³, so pH = 4.46. The acid is in excess, so the pH is below the pKa of 4.76.","w":["This treats the acid alone as a weak acid and ignores the ethanoate ions already present from the salt.","","This is the pKa, which would only be the pH if [acid] = [salt].","The ratio is inverted, using [salt] ÷ [acid]; with more acid than salt, pH is below pKa."]}
{"id":"ch-134","s":"chem","t":"3.1.12","lv":1,"q":"0.100 mol dm⁻³ ethanoic acid is titrated with 0.100 mol dm⁻³ sodium hydroxide. Which indicator is suitable, and why?","o":["Methyl orange (3.1–4.4), as the salt formed makes the end point below pH 7","Either methyl orange or phenolphthalein, as both change within the vertical section","Neither, as only a pH meter can detect the end point of a weak acid titration","Phenolphthalein (8.3–10.0), as the equivalence point is above pH 7"],"a":3,"x":"Sodium ethanoate is basic through hydrolysis, so the equivalence point is above pH 7. The steep section starts near pH 7 and phenolphthalein changes within it. Methyl orange changes before the steep section.","w":["The sodium ethanoate formed is basic, so the equivalence point is above pH 7, not below.","Methyl orange changes colour at pH 3.1–4.4, well before the steep section of a weak acid–strong base curve.","Phenolphthalein is suitable for this titration, so a pH meter is not the only option.",""]}
{"id":"ch-135","s":"chem","t":"3.1.12","lv":3,"q":"25.0 cm³ of 0.200 mol dm⁻³ ethanoic acid is mixed with 10.0 cm³ of 0.200 mol dm⁻³ NaOH. Ka = 1.74 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³. What is the pH of the mixture?","o":["4.94","4.76","4.58","1.07"],"a":2,"x":"Acid = 5.00 mmol, NaOH = 2.00 mmol. Remaining acid = 3.00 mmol, ethanoate formed = 2.00 mmol. [H⁺] = Ka × 3.00 ÷ 2.00 = 2.61 × 10⁻⁵, so pH = 4.58. Volumes cancel in the ratio.","w":["The ratio is inverted (2.00 ÷ 3.00); there is more acid than salt, so the pH is below the pKa.","This is the pKa, which applies only at half-neutralisation; here only 40% of the acid has reacted.","","This treats the leftover 3.00 mmol in 35.0 cm³ as a strong acid, ignoring the buffer."]}
{"id":"ch-136","s":"chem","t":"3.2.2","lv":1,"q":"In the test for sulfate ions, dilute hydrochloric acid is added before aqueous barium chloride. What is the purpose of adding the acid?","o":["To increase the concentration of Ba²⁺ ions so that the precipitate forms faster","To react with any carbonate ions, which would otherwise give a white precipitate of BaCO₃","To oxidise any sulfite ions to sulfate ions so that they are also detected","To stop barium chloride itself forming a white precipitate with the solution"],"a":1,"x":"Barium carbonate and barium sulfite are also insoluble white solids, so they would give a false positive. Dilute HCl reacts with carbonate ions and removes them, so only BaSO₄ can precipitate. The acid does not affect Ba²⁺ concentration.","w":["The acid does not raise Ba²⁺ concentration; it removes interfering ions, and the rate of precipitation is not the point.","","HCl is not an oxidising agent and would not turn sulfite into sulfate; it removes carbonate and sulfite instead.","Barium chloride is soluble, so it would not precipitate anyway; the interfering precipitate is BaCO₃."]}
{"id":"ch-137","s":"chem","t":"3.2.2","lv":2,"q":"Equal volumes of dilute aqueous sodium hydroxide are added separately to dilute solutions of magnesium nitrate and barium nitrate. Which observation is correct?","o":["No precipitate with either, as all Group 2 hydroxides are soluble","White precipitate with both, as all Group 2 hydroxides are insoluble","White precipitate with Ba²⁺ only, as hydroxide solubility decreases down the group","White precipitate with Mg²⁺ only, as hydroxide solubility increases down the group"],"a":3,"x":"Group 2 hydroxide solubility increases down the group. Mg(OH)₂ is almost insoluble and precipitates, while Ba(OH)₂ is soluble, so no solid forms. The trend is opposite to that for the sulfates.","w":["Mg(OH)₂ is almost insoluble, so a precipitate forms; the hydroxides are not all soluble.","Ba(OH)₂ is soluble, so the hydroxides are not all insoluble; solubility rises down the group.","This reverses the trend by applying the sulfate pattern to the hydroxides; hydroxide solubility increases down the group.",""]}
{"id":"ch-138","s":"chem","t":"3.2.2","lv":3,"q":"A power station emits 640 kg of SO₂ per hour (Mr = 64.0). Limestone, CaCO₃ (Mr = 100.1), removes 80% of it by CaCO₃ + SO₂ → CaSO₃ + CO₂. What mass of CaCO₃ is needed per hour?","o":["801 kg","1.00 × 10³ kg","512 kg","641 kg"],"a":0,"x":"SO₂ removed = 0.80 × 640 = 512 kg = 8.00 kmol. The ratio is 1:1, so 8.00 kmol CaCO₃ × 100.1 = 801 kg. The traps are ignoring the 80% and equating the masses of the two substances.","w":["","This ignores the 80% removal and treats all 640 kg of SO₂ as being removed.","This is the mass of SO₂ removed; it forgets to convert to moles and use the Mr of CaCO₃.","This applies the 80% twice, once to the SO₂ and again to the CaCO₃ mass."]}
{"id":"ch-139","s":"chem","t":"3.2.3","lv":1,"q":"In the test for halide ions, dilute nitric acid is added to the unknown solution before aqueous silver nitrate. What is the purpose of the nitric acid?","o":["To dissolve the silver halide so that the precipitate colour can be seen","To oxidise the halide ions so that they can react with silver ions","To remove carbonate and hydroxide ions that would also precipitate with Ag⁺","To lower the pH so that the silver halide precipitates faster"],"a":2,"x":"Carbonate and hydroxide ions would form Ag₂CO₃ or Ag₂O precipitates with silver ions and mask the result. Dilute nitric acid removes them first. The acid does not dissolve, oxidise or speed up anything.","w":["The acid is there to prevent false precipitates; the silver halide is meant to form and stay as a solid.","Halide ions need no oxidising to precipitate; the reaction is Ag⁺ + X⁻ → AgX, with no change in oxidation state.","","Silver halide precipitation is not controlled by pH; the acid removes interfering ions and does not speed up the reaction."]}
{"id":"ch-140","s":"chem","t":"3.2.3","lv":2,"q":"Chlorine reacts with hot, concentrated NaOH: 3Cl₂ + 6NaOH → 5NaCl + NaClO₃ + 3H₂O. What mass of NaClO₃ (Mᵣ = 106.5) forms from 0.060 mol of Cl₂, assuming complete reaction?","o":["6.39 g","2.13 g","1.07 g","5.85 g"],"a":1,"x":"The mole ratio Cl₂ : NaClO₃ is 3 : 1, so 0.060 mol Cl₂ gives 0.020 mol NaClO₃. Mass = 0.020 × 106.5 = 2.13 g. Do not use a 1 : 1 ratio, as in the cold NaOH reaction.","w":["This uses a 1 : 1 ratio (0.060 mol), ignoring the 3 : 1 ratio in the balanced equation.","","This divides by 6, the NaOH coefficient, instead of 3, the Cl₂ coefficient, giving only 0.010 mol.","This is the mass of the NaCl product (0.10 mol × 58.5 g mol⁻¹), not NaClO₃."]}
{"id":"ch-141","s":"chem","t":"3.2.4","lv":1,"q":"Which equation correctly represents the reaction of sodium with cold water?","o":["2Na + H₂O → Na₂O + H₂","2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂","Na + H₂O → NaOH + H₂","2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + O₂"],"a":1,"x":"Sodium reacts vigorously with cold water to give hydrogen gas and sodium hydroxide, a strongly alkaline solution. The balanced equation needs 2Na and 2H₂O. The traps are an unbalanced equation or the wrong gas.","w":["Sodium oxide is not the product with water; sodium forms NaOH and H₂.","","Not balanced: there are 2 H atoms on the left but 3 on the right.","Oxygen is not released; hydrogen is the gas formed when sodium reacts with water."]}
{"id":"ch-142","s":"chem","t":"3.2.4","lv":1,"q":"Aluminium oxide is described as amphoteric. Which observation shows this?","o":["It reacts with hydrochloric acid but does not dissolve in aqueous sodium hydroxide","It dissolves in water to give a neutral solution of pH about 7","It has a very high melting point and does not conduct when solid","It dissolves in both dilute hydrochloric acid and aqueous sodium hydroxide"],"a":3,"x":"An amphoteric oxide reacts with both acids and bases. Al₂O₃ reacts with HCl as a base and with NaOH(aq) as an acid, forming a soluble aluminate. The trap is confusing amphoteric with neutral.","w":["This shows only basic behaviour; an amphoteric oxide must also react with alkali.","Al₂O₃ is insoluble in water; being amphoteric does not mean neutral in water.","This describes giant ionic lattice properties, not acid–base behaviour.",""]}
{"id":"ch-143","s":"chem","t":"3.2.4","lv":2,"q":"0.0150 mol of P₄O₁₀ reacts completely with water: P₄O₁₀ + 6H₂O → 4H₃PO₄. The solution is made up to 500 cm³. What is the concentration of H₃PO₄?","o":["0.120 mol dm⁻³","0.0300 mol dm⁻³","0.0600 mol dm⁻³","0.180 mol dm⁻³"],"a":0,"x":"Moles H₃PO₄ = 4 × 0.0150 = 0.0600 mol. Concentration = 0.0600 ÷ 0.500 dm³ = 0.120 mol dm⁻³. The traps are ignoring the 1:4 ratio or forgetting to convert 500 cm³ to dm³.","w":["","Ignored the 1:4 mole ratio: 0.0150 ÷ 0.500 gives this, but 4 mol of acid form per mol of P₄O₁₀.","Divided by 1 dm³ instead of 0.500 dm³, forgetting the volume of the solution.","Used the 6 from H₂O as the ratio; P₄O₁₀ to H₃PO₄ is 1:4."]}
{"id":"ch-144","s":"chem","t":"3.2.4","lv":2,"q":"Why does silicon(IV) oxide, SiO₂, have a much higher melting point than sulfur dioxide, SO₂?","o":["Si–O bonds are ionic, so much energy is needed to separate the ions","SiO₂ has stronger London forces between its molecules than SO₂ has","Melting SiO₂ breaks strong covalent bonds; SO₂ has weak intermolecular forces","The S=O bonds in SO₂ are weaker than Si–O bonds, so they break on melting"],"a":2,"x":"SiO₂ is a giant covalent lattice, so melting requires breaking many strong covalent bonds. SO₂ is simple molecular and only weak intermolecular forces are overcome. The trap is thinking covalent bonds break when a molecular substance melts.","w":["Si–O bonds are polar covalent, and SiO₂ is a giant covalent lattice, not ionic.","SiO₂ has no separate molecules; it is a giant lattice, so London forces are not the explanation.","","Melting SO₂ does not break S=O bonds; only intermolecular forces are overcome."]}
{"id":"ch-145","s":"chem","t":"3.2.4","lv":2,"q":"Na₂O gives a solution of pH about 13–14 in water, but MgO gives only about pH 9–10. What is the best explanation?","o":["Mg(OH)₂ is only sparingly soluble, so the solution has a low concentration of OH⁻","MgO is covalent, so it cannot form hydroxide ions with water","MgO has a higher melting point, so it does not react with water at all","MgO is an acidic oxide that partly neutralises the hydroxide formed"],"a":0,"x":"Both oxides are basic. MgO reacts slightly with water to form Mg(OH)₂, which is poorly soluble, so [OH⁻] stays low. Na₂O forms very soluble NaOH. The trap is saying MgO does not react or is covalent.","w":["","MgO is a giant ionic lattice, and the oxide ion gives hydroxide ions with water.","MgO does react slightly with water, giving a weakly alkaline solution, so it is not unreactive.","MgO is a basic oxide, not acidic; the low pH is due to low solubility."]}
{"id":"ch-146","s":"chem","t":"3.2.4","lv":3,"q":"1.55 g of Na₂O (Mr = 62.0) is added to water and made up to 500 cm³ of solution at 298 K (Kw = 1.00 × 10⁻¹⁴ mol² dm⁻⁶). What is the pH of the solution?","o":["12.7","13.3","1.0","13.0"],"a":3,"x":"Moles Na₂O = 1.55 ÷ 62.0 = 0.0250. Na₂O + H₂O → 2NaOH gives 0.0500 mol OH⁻ in 0.500 dm³, so [OH⁻] = 0.100 mol dm⁻³. pOH = 1, so pH = 13.0. The trap is missing the factor of 2.","w":["Forgot that each Na₂O gives 2 OH⁻, using [OH⁻] = 0.0500 mol dm⁻³.","Used 250 cm³ instead of 500 cm³, giving [OH⁻] = 0.200 mol dm⁻³.","Treated 0.100 mol dm⁻³ as [H⁺], but Na₂O gives an alkaline solution.",""]}
{"id":"ch-147","s":"chem","t":"3.2.5","lv":1,"q":"Which of these elements is a transition metal, because it forms at least one ion with an incomplete d sub-shell?","o":["Scandium, which forms Sc³⁺","Zinc, which forms Zn²⁺","Copper, which forms Cu²⁺","Calcium, which forms Ca²⁺"],"a":2,"x":"Cu²⁺ is 3d⁹, an incomplete d sub-shell. Sc³⁺ has an empty 3d and Zn²⁺ a full 3d¹⁰, so neither qualifies. Being in the d-block is not enough. Calcium is in the s-block.","w":["Sc³⁺ has no d electrons (3d⁰), so its only common ion has an empty, not incomplete, d sub-shell.","Zn²⁺ is 3d¹⁰, a full d sub-shell; zinc is d-block but not a transition metal.","","Calcium is an s-block element and Ca²⁺ has no d electrons; this ignores the definition."]}
{"id":"ch-148","s":"chem","t":"3.2.5","lv":1,"q":"What are the shape and the coordination number of the complex ion [CuCl₄]²⁻?","o":["Tetrahedral; coordination number 4","Square planar; coordination number 4","Octahedral; coordination number 6","Tetrahedral; coordination number 2"],"a":0,"x":"Four large Cl⁻ ligands are bonded to Cu²⁺, so the coordination number is 4. Large ligands such as Cl⁻ give a tetrahedral shape; six would not fit round the metal ion.","w":["","Square planar is typical of Pt²⁺ complexes such as cisplatin; four large Cl⁻ ligands round Cu²⁺ give a tetrahedral shape.","Assumes the usual octahedral shape, but only four Cl⁻ ligands are present, so the coordination number is 4.","Confuses the 2− charge with the coordination number, which counts the dative bonds (four)."]}
{"id":"ch-149","s":"chem","t":"3.2.5","lv":2,"q":"Aqueous solutions of [Zn(H₂O)₆]²⁺ are colourless, whereas [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ is blue. Which statement explains this?","o":["Zn²⁺ has an empty 3d sub-shell, so no electrons can be promoted","Zn²⁺ absorbs all wavelengths of visible light strongly","Water is a colourless ligand, so it cannot give coloured complexes with zinc","Zn²⁺ has a full 3d sub-shell, so no d–d electron promotion can occur"],"a":3,"x":"Colour comes from d electrons absorbing visible light and moving to a higher d orbital. Zn²⁺ is 3d¹⁰: the d orbitals are full, so no d–d transition is possible. It is not empty, and water also gives the blue Cu²⁺ complex.","w":["Zn²⁺ is 3d¹⁰, not empty; the slip is confusing a full d sub-shell with an empty one.","If it absorbed all visible light it would look black, not colourless; the real cause is no d–d transition.","Water ligands also give blue [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺, so ligand colour is not the reason; the d sub-shell is the cause.",""]}
{"id":"ch-150","s":"chem","t":"3.2.5","lv":2,"q":"A complex absorbs light of wavelength 600 nm. Using ΔE = hc/λ (h = 6.63×10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3.00×10⁸ m s⁻¹), what is the d-orbital energy gap for one ion?","o":["3.3×10⁻²⁸ J","3.3×10⁻¹⁹ J","2.0×10⁵ J","3.3×10⁻²² J"],"a":1,"x":"Convert 600 nm to 6.00×10⁻⁷ m: ΔE = (6.63×10⁻³⁴ × 3.00×10⁸) ÷ 6.00×10⁻⁷ = 3.3×10⁻¹⁹ J per ion. Forgetting to convert nm to m, or multiplying by L, gives the wrong answers.","w":["Used 600 instead of 6.00×10⁻⁷ m, forgetting to convert nanometres to metres.","","This is the energy per mole (multiplied by 6.02×10²³), but the question asks for one ion.","Converted nm to m wrongly (10⁻⁶ instead of 10⁻⁹), a unit-conversion slip."]}
{"id":"ch-151","s":"chem","t":"3.2.5","lv":2,"q":"Yellow VO₂⁺ in acid is reduced by excess zinc until the solution is violet. Which ion gives the violet colour, and what is the oxidation state of vanadium in it?","o":["VO²⁺, +4","V³⁺, +3","VO₂⁺, +5","V²⁺, +2"],"a":3,"x":"Zinc reduces vanadium stepwise: VO₂⁺ (+5, yellow), VO²⁺ (+4, blue), V³⁺ (+3, green), then V²⁺ (+2, violet). Excess zinc takes it all the way to +2. Stopping at +4 or +3 gives blue or green.","w":["VO²⁺ is blue, an intermediate stage; excess zinc reduces beyond +4.","V³⁺ is green, an intermediate stage; further reduction to V²⁺ is needed for violet.","VO₂⁺ is the yellow starting ion and has not been reduced at all.",""]}
{"id":"ch-152","s":"chem","t":"3.2.5","lv":3,"q":"Fe²⁺ ions catalyse the reaction between I⁻ and S₂O₈²⁻ in solution. Which statement best explains why Fe²⁺ is an effective catalyst here?","o":["It is oxidised by S₂O₈²⁻ then reduced by I⁻, so the ions react via an oppositely charged ion","It provides a solid surface on which both negative ions adsorb and react","It makes the reaction more exothermic, so the equilibrium shifts towards the products","It is a stronger oxidising agent than S₂O₈²⁻ and oxidises I⁻ directly"],"a":0,"x":"Fe²⁺ can change oxidation state: S₂O₈²⁻ oxidises Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺, then I⁻ reduces Fe³⁺ back to Fe²⁺. This avoids repulsion between two negative ions and gives a lower-activation-energy route. The catalyst is regenerated.","w":["","This describes a heterogeneous catalyst, but Fe²⁺ is in the same aqueous phase as the reactants, so it is homogeneous.","Catalysts do not change ΔH or the equilibrium position; they only lower the activation energy.","Fe³⁺ is the oxidant for I⁻, not Fe²⁺, and Fe²⁺ is regenerated; it is not simply a stronger oxidising agent."]}
{"id":"ch-153","s":"chem","t":"3.2.6","lv":1,"q":"Which aqueous metal ion forms a white precipitate with NaOH(aq) that dissolves in excess NaOH(aq) to give a colourless solution?","o":["Mg²⁺","Al³⁺","Ca²⁺","Cr³⁺"],"a":1,"x":"Al(OH)₃ is amphoteric, so it dissolves in excess hydroxide as [Al(OH)₄]⁻. Mg(OH)₂ is also white but does not dissolve. Cr(OH)₃ dissolves, but it is green, not white.","w":["Mg(OH)₂ is white but not amphoteric, so it stays undissolved in excess NaOH.","","Ca(OH)₂ is a white solid that does not dissolve in excess NaOH; only amphoteric hydroxides do.","Cr(OH)₃ does dissolve in excess NaOH, but the precipitate and the solution are both green."]}
{"id":"ch-154","s":"chem","t":"3.2.6","lv":1,"q":"A solution of [Fe(H₂O)₆]³⁺ is much more acidic than one of [Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺ of the same concentration. What is the best explanation?","o":["Fe³⁺ binds more water ligands than Fe²⁺ does, so more water is available to donate H⁺","Fe³⁺ has more electrons than Fe²⁺, so it repels the O–H bonding electrons more","Fe³⁺ is a larger ion than Fe²⁺, so it hydrogen-bonds more strongly to water","Fe³⁺ has a higher charge density, so it polarises the O–H bonds in the water ligands more"],"a":3,"x":"The smaller, more highly charged Fe³⁺ has a greater charge density. It draws electron density from the ligand water, weakening O–H bonds so H⁺ is released more easily. Both ions have six ligands.","w":["Both ions are octahedral with six water ligands, so the number of ligands is not the difference.","Fe³⁺ has fewer electrons than Fe²⁺, not more, and electron count is not the reason for acidity.","Fe³⁺ is smaller than Fe²⁺ because it has lost another electron, so this reverses the size trend.",""]}
{"id":"ch-155","s":"chem","t":"3.2.6","lv":2,"q":"Aqueous sodium carbonate is added to a solution containing [Fe(H₂O)₆]³⁺ ions. What is observed?","o":["A brown precipitate and effervescence of a colourless gas","A brown precipitate only, as iron(III) carbonate forms","A green precipitate and effervescence of a colourless gas","A brown precipitate that dissolves in excess carbonate to give a brown solution"],"a":0,"x":"The 3+ aqua ion is acidic enough to react with CO₃²⁻, giving CO₂ and a brown Fe(H₂O)₃(OH)₃ precipitate. Iron(III) carbonate does not form. M²⁺ ions such as Cu²⁺ give carbonate precipitates with no gas.","w":["","Fe₂(CO₃)₃ is not formed; the acidic Fe³⁺ ion reacts with carbonate to give CO₂ as well, so gas is seen.","A green precipitate is Fe²⁺ chemistry; the Fe³⁺ hydroxide precipitate is brown.","Fe(OH)₃ is not amphoteric and does not dissolve in excess carbonate; this confuses it with Al³⁺ in excess NaOH."]}
{"id":"ch-156","s":"chem","t":"3.2.6","lv":2,"q":"Dilute ammonia solution is added dropwise, and then in excess, to aqueous copper(II) sulfate. What is seen?","o":["A pale blue precipitate that does not dissolve in excess ammonia","A pale blue precipitate that dissolves in excess ammonia to give a colourless solution","A pale blue precipitate that dissolves in excess ammonia to give a deep blue solution","A blue-green precipitate that turns brown on standing in air"],"a":2,"x":"A little NH₃ acts as a base, giving pale blue Cu(H₂O)₄(OH)₂. Excess NH₃ exchanges ligands to form deep blue [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺. The trap is expecting it to stay insoluble, as it does with NaOH.","w":["Cu(OH)₂ is insoluble in excess NaOH, but in excess ammonia it dissolves by ligand exchange.","The copper ammine complex is deep blue; Cu²⁺ complexes are not colourless, as there is a d-d transition.","","Green precipitates browning in air belong to iron(II) hydroxide chemistry, not copper(II) with ammonia."]}
{"id":"ch-157","s":"chem","t":"3.2.6","lv":2,"q":"Solution X gives a green precipitate with NaOH(aq), insoluble in excess. The precipitate darkens to brown at its surface on standing in air. Which ion is present in X?","o":["Fe³⁺","Fe²⁺","Cr³⁺","Cu²⁺"],"a":1,"x":"Fe²⁺ gives green Fe(OH)₂, which is oxidised by air to brown Fe(OH)₃ at the surface. Fe³⁺ gives brown immediately, Cr³⁺ gives a green precipitate that dissolves in excess, and Cu²⁺ gives a blue one.","w":["Fe³⁺ gives a brown precipitate straight away, not a green one that browns later.","","Cr(OH)₃ is green but dissolves in excess NaOH and does not turn brown in air.","Cu²⁺ gives a pale blue precipitate that stays blue; it is not green."]}
{"id":"ch-158","s":"chem","t":"3.2.6","lv":3,"q":"Aqueous Na₂CO₃ added to aqueous Al³⁺ gives a white precipitate and a colourless gas. Which equation correctly represents the reaction?","o":["[Al(H₂O)₆]³⁺ + CO₃²⁻ → Al(H₂O)₃(OH)₃ + CO₂ + H₂O","2[Al(H₂O)₆]³⁺ + 3CO₃²⁻ → Al₂(CO₃)₃ + 12H₂O","2[Al(H₂O)₆]³⁺ + 3CO₃²⁻ → 2Al(H₂O)₃(OH)₃ + 3CO₂ + 6H₂O","2[Al(H₂O)₆]³⁺ + 3CO₃²⁻ → 2Al(H₂O)₃(OH)₃ + 3CO₂ + 3H₂O"],"a":3,"x":"Each Al³⁺ aqua ion loses three H⁺, which react with carbonate to give CO₂ and water. Check: 24 H and 21 O on both sides, charge 0 on both. Al₂(CO₃)₃ does not form, and that equation gives no gas.","w":["This is not balanced: the charge is +1 on the left and 0 on the right, and the atoms do not balance.","This balances but is wrong chemically: aluminium carbonate does not form, and no CO₂ would be produced.","The water is miscounted: with 6H₂O the right side has 30 H and 24 O, but the left has 24 H and 21 O.",""]}
{"id":"ch-159","s":"chem","t":"3.3.2","lv":1,"q":"Which row correctly gives the conditions and the main products of catalytic cracking of long-chain alkanes in industry?","o":["No catalyst, about 900 °C, high pressure; branched and cyclic alkanes, aromatics","Zeolite, about 450 °C, slight pressure; mainly unchanged long-chain alkanes","Zeolite, about 450 °C, slight pressure; branched and cyclic alkanes, aromatics","Zeolite, about 20 °C, high pressure; branched and cyclic alkanes, aromatics"],"a":2,"x":"Catalytic cracking uses a zeolite catalyst at about 450 °C and only slight pressure. It gives branched alkanes, cycloalkanes and aromatics for motor fuels. The high-pressure, very hot, catalyst-free conditions describe thermal cracking.","w":["These are thermal cracking conditions, which mainly give alkenes, so the conditions and products are mixed up.","Cracking breaks long chains into shorter ones, so long-chain alkanes cannot be the main products.","","About 20 °C is far too cold to break C–C bonds, and the pressure is slight, not high."]}
{"id":"ch-160","s":"chem","t":"3.3.2","lv":3,"q":"Octane burns in a limited air supply to give carbon monoxide and carbon dioxide in a 1 : 1 mole ratio, plus water only. How many moles of O₂ react with each mole of C₈H₁₈?","o":["8.5","12.5","21","10.5"],"a":3,"x":"Products per mole of octane: 4CO + 4CO₂ + 9H₂O. Oxygen atoms = 4 + 8 + 9 = 21. O₂ molecules = 21 ÷ 2 = 10.5. Trap: forgetting that O₂ has two atoms.","w":["8.5 is the value if all the carbon formed only CO, not a 1 : 1 mixture of CO and CO₂.","12.5 is the value for complete combustion, with all the carbon going to CO₂.","21 counts oxygen atoms, not O₂ molecules, so it is twice too large.",""]}
{"id":"ch-161","s":"chem","t":"3.3.2","lv":2,"q":"Chlorination of ethane in UV light gives a small amount of butane as a by-product. Which step produces the butane?","o":["CH₃CH₂· + CH₃CH₂· → CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃","CH₃CH₂· + Cl₂ → CH₃CH₂Cl + Cl·","CH₃CH₂· + CH₃· → CH₃CH₂CH₃","CH₃CH₃ + Cl· → CH₃CH₂· + HCl"],"a":0,"x":"Butane has four carbons, so two ethyl radicals must join. This termination step uses up two radicals and makes no new one. Propagation steps produce chloroethane or ethyl radicals, not butane.","w":["","This propagation step makes chloroethane, the main product, and regenerates Cl·, so no C–C bond forms.","This termination joins ethyl and methyl radicals to give propane (3 carbons), not butane.","This propagation step forms an ethyl radical and HCl; it does not make a C₄ molecule."]}
{"id":"ch-162","s":"chem","t":"3.3.3","lv":2,"q":"Equal amounts of 1-chlorobutane, 1-bromobutane and 1-iodobutane are warmed with aqueous silver nitrate in ethanol. Which statement correctly explains the order in which precipitates form?","o":["Chloro is fastest, because the C–Cl bond is the most polar","Iodo is fastest, because the C–I bond has the lowest bond enthalpy","Iodo is fastest, because the C–I bond is the most polar","All form at the same rate, because water is the nucleophile in each"],"a":1,"x":"Hydrolysis rate depends on how easily the C–X bond breaks. C–I is the weakest bond, so the iodoalkane reacts fastest and gives the yellow AgI precipitate first. Polarity is the trap: it does not control the rate.","w":["C–Cl is the most polar, but rate depends on bond strength, not polarity; C–Cl is the strongest bond so it is slowest.","","C–I is actually the least polar of the three, as iodine is closest to carbon in electronegativity.","The nucleophile is the same, but the rate still depends on how easily each C–X bond breaks."]}
{"id":"ch-163","s":"chem","t":"3.3.3","lv":1,"q":"1-bromopropane is heated under reflux with potassium cyanide in aqueous ethanol. What is the main organic product?","o":["CH₃CH₂CH₂OH","CH₃CH₂CN","CH₃CH=CH₂","CH₃CH₂CH₂CN"],"a":3,"x":"CN⁻ is a nucleophile and replaces Br⁻ by nucleophilic substitution. The CN carbon is added to the three-carbon chain, giving butanenitrile, CH₃CH₂CH₂CN. The trap is forgetting that the chain gets one carbon longer.","w":["This would need OH⁻ as the nucleophile; the nucleophile here is CN⁻, so a nitrile forms, not an alcohol.","This has only three carbons in total; the cyanide carbon adds to the propyl chain, so the product has four.","Elimination to propene needs a strong base in ethanol; cyanide here substitutes as a nucleophile.",""]}
{"id":"ch-164","s":"chem","t":"3.3.3","lv":3,"q":"2-bromobutane is heated under reflux with ethanolic KOH. How many different alkenes, counting E/Z isomers as different, can form by elimination of HBr?","o":["3","1","2","4"],"a":0,"x":"Removing H from C1 gives but-1-ene. Removing H from C3 gives but-2-ene, which exists as E and Z isomers. That is three alkenes. The trap is forgetting the E/Z pair.","w":["","Only one alkene would form if H could be lost from just one side; there are hydrogens on both C1 and C3.","This counts but-1-ene and but-2-ene but misses that but-2-ene has E and Z stereoisomers.","Only two carbons next to C–Br have hydrogens, giving three alkenes in total; there is no fourth."]}
{"id":"ch-165","s":"chem","t":"3.3.4","lv":1,"q":"Which statement correctly describes the π bond in an alkene such as ethene?","o":["Head-on overlap of p orbitals; electron density between the carbon nuclei","Sideways overlap of p orbitals; electron density above and below the plane","Sideways overlap of p orbitals; electron density within the molecular plane","Head-on overlap of s orbitals; electron density around each carbon nucleus"],"a":1,"x":"A π bond forms by sideways overlap of p orbitals. Its electron density lies above and below the plane of the molecule, and this exposed region attracts electrophiles. The trap is mixing it up with the σ bond, which forms by head-on overlap.","w":["Head-on overlap along the C–C axis describes the σ bond, not the π bond.","","Sideways overlap is right, but π electron density is above and below the plane, not in it.","The π bond is not made from s orbitals; p orbitals overlap sideways to form it."]}
{"id":"ch-166","s":"chem","t":"3.3.4","lv":2,"q":"2-methylbut-1-ene, CH₂=C(CH₃)CH₂CH₃, reacts with HBr. What is the major product?","o":["1-bromo-2-methylbutane","2-bromo-3-methylbutane","2-bromo-2-methylbutane","1,2-dibromo-2-methylbutane"],"a":2,"x":"H⁺ adds to the CH₂ carbon, giving a tertiary carbocation at C2, which is the most stable. Br⁻ then attacks it to give 2-bromo-2-methylbutane. The primary carbocation route gives only a minor product.","w":["This comes from the less stable primary carbocation, so it is only the minor product.","Here the Br is on a carbon that was not part of the C=C, which this mechanism cannot give.","","HBr adds only one Br atom; two bromines would need Br₂, not HBr."]}
{"id":"ch-167","s":"chem","t":"3.3.4","lv":2,"q":"Which is the repeat unit of the addition polymer formed from but-2-ene, CH₃CH=CHCH₃?","o":["–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–CH₂–","–CH₂–CH(CH₂CH₃)–","–CH(CH₃)=CH(CH₃)–","–CH(CH₃)–CH(CH₃)–"],"a":3,"x":"In addition polymerisation the C=C opens, so both alkene carbons join the single-bonded backbone. The two CH₃ groups stay as side chains, giving –CH(CH₃)–CH(CH₃)–. The trap is moving or removing the side groups.","w":["This puts all four carbons in the backbone and loses the side groups, which rearranges the carbon skeleton.","This is the repeat unit of but-1-ene, not but-2-ene, so the monomer has been misread.","The C=C opens during polymerisation, so no double bond remains in the backbone.",""]}
{"id":"ch-168","s":"chem","t":"3.3.5","lv":1,"q":"Which alcohol would show no colour change when warmed with acidified potassium dichromate(VI)?","o":["Propan-2-ol","Butan-1-ol","2-methylpropan-2-ol","2-methylpropan-1-ol"],"a":2,"x":"2-methylpropan-2-ol is tertiary. The carbon bearing OH has no hydrogen atom, so it cannot be oxidised and the solution stays orange. Branching elsewhere does not matter; only the C–OH carbon counts.","w":["Propan-2-ol is secondary and is oxidised to propanone, so the orange solution turns green.","Butan-1-ol is primary and is oxidised to butanal or butanoic acid, so the solution turns green.","","Despite the branch, this is primary (C–OH carbon has two H atoms), so it is oxidised; judging by branching is the slip."]}
{"id":"ch-169","s":"chem","t":"3.3.5","lv":2,"q":"Propanal is made by gently heating propan-1-ol with acidified dichromate and distilling it off as it forms. Why does this stop further oxidation?","o":["Propanal has no O–H so cannot hydrogen bond; it boils lower and distils out","Propanal is more polar than propan-1-ol, so it evaporates more easily","Propanal has a much lower Mr than propan-1-ol, so its London forces are weaker","Propanal is a gas at room temperature, so it escapes without heating"],"a":0,"x":"Propan-1-ol molecules hydrogen bond through O–H, but propanal cannot. Propanal boils lower (about 48 °C versus 97 °C), so it distils out before it can be oxidised further to propanoic acid. Polarity and Mr are the traps.","w":["","Polarity is not the reason: the alcohol's hydrogen bonding is stronger, so propanal is the more volatile one.","Mr values are almost equal (58 and 60); the real difference is hydrogen bonding in the alcohol.","Propanal is a liquid at room temperature (boiling point about 48 °C), so it must be heated to distil."]}
{"id":"ch-170","s":"chem","t":"3.3.5","lv":3,"q":"7.40 g of butan-2-ol (Mr 74.0) is oxidised to butanone (Mr 72.0) with a 60% yield. What mass of butanone is obtained?","o":["4.44 g","7.20 g","12.0 g","4.32 g"],"a":3,"x":"Moles of alcohol = 7.40/74.0 = 0.100 mol, giving 0.100 mol butanone at 100%. Mass = 0.100 × 72.0 = 7.20 g; at 60% yield this is 4.32 g. Use the Mr of the product, not the reactant.","w":["Uses the Mr of butan-2-ol (74.0) for the product mass instead of 72.0 for butanone.","This is the theoretical yield; the 60% yield has not been applied.","Divides by 0.60 instead of multiplying, which gives a mass above the theoretical maximum.",""]}
{"id":"ch-171","s":"chem","t":"3.3.6","lv":1,"q":"A few drops of an unknown liquid are warmed with Tollens' reagent in a water bath and a silver mirror forms. Which compound could the liquid be?","o":["Propanone, CH₃COCH₃","Propan-2-ol, CH₃CH(OH)CH₃","Propanal, CH₃CH₂CHO","Propanoic acid, CH₃CH₂COOH"],"a":2,"x":"Tollens' reagent is reduced to silver by aldehydes, which are easily oxidised. Ketones do not react, and neither do secondary alcohols or carboxylic acids, which are not oxidised by it. The trap is thinking any C=O compound gives a mirror.","w":["A ketone has a C=O group but cannot be oxidised further by Tollens' reagent, so it gives no mirror.","A secondary alcohol is not oxidised by Tollens' reagent, so no silver forms; it needs acidified dichromate.","","A carboxylic acid is already fully oxidised, so it cannot reduce Tollens' reagent to silver."]}
{"id":"ch-172","s":"chem","t":"3.3.6","lv":2,"q":"An alcohol is warmed with acidified potassium dichromate(VI) and the mixture stays orange. Which alcohol was it?","o":["2-methylpropan-2-ol","Butan-2-ol","Butan-1-ol","2-methylpropan-1-ol"],"a":0,"x":"No colour change means the alcohol is not oxidised, so it is tertiary. 2-methylpropan-2-ol is tertiary. Primary and secondary alcohols reduce orange Cr₂O₇²⁻ to green Cr³⁺. The trap is picking a branched alcohol that is actually primary.","w":["","Butan-2-ol is secondary and is oxidised to butanone, so the mixture would turn green.","Butan-1-ol is primary and is oxidised, so the mixture would turn green.","2-methylpropan-1-ol is branched but still primary, so it is oxidised and the mixture turns green."]}
{"id":"ch-173","s":"chem","t":"3.3.6","lv":2,"q":"In the test for a halogenoalkane, it is warmed with NaOH(aq), then dilute nitric acid is added before silver nitrate. Why is the nitric acid added?","o":["To hydrolyse the halogenoalkane more quickly","To oxidise the halide ions so they react with silver","To dissolve the silver halide precipitate once it forms","To neutralise excess OH⁻, which would form a precipitate with Ag⁺"],"a":3,"x":"Excess hydroxide would react with silver ions to give a brown Ag₂O precipitate that hides the halide result. Acidifying removes OH⁻ first. The trap is thinking the acid takes part in the hydrolysis or in the precipitation.","w":["Hydrolysis is done by the hot NaOH in the earlier step; the acid is added afterwards.","Halide ions are already the ions that precipitate with Ag⁺; no oxidation is needed.","The acid would not dissolve the precipitate; the aim is to see a precipitate clearly.",""]}
{"id":"ch-174","s":"chem","t":"3.3.6","lv":3,"q":"A compound has M⁺ at m/z 72. Its IR spectrum has a strong sharp peak near 1715 cm⁻¹ and no broad absorption above 2500 cm⁻¹. It gives no silver mirror with Tollens' reagent. What is it?","o":["Butanal, CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO","Butanone, CH₃COCH₂CH₃","But-3-en-1-ol, CH₂=CHCH₂CH₂OH","Pentan-2-one, CH₃COCH₂CH₂CH₃"],"a":1,"x":"Mr 72 fits C₄H₈O. The 1715 cm⁻¹ peak shows C=O, with no O–H. No mirror rules out an aldehyde, so it is a ketone: butanone. Butanal has the same Mr but gives a mirror.","w":["Butanal also has Mr 72 and C=O, but as an aldehyde it would give a silver mirror.","","But-3-en-1-ol has Mr 72, but it has O–H and no C=O, so no sharp 1715 cm⁻¹ peak.","Pentan-2-one is a ketone, but its Mr is 86, not 72."]}
{"id":"ch-175","s":"chem","t":"3.3.7","lv":1,"q":"Which alcohol contains a chiral centre and so shows optical isomerism?","o":["Propan-2-ol","Butan-2-ol","2-methylpropan-1-ol","Pentan-3-ol"],"a":1,"x":"In butan-2-ol, CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₃, carbon 2 bonds to four different groups: H, OH, CH₃ and C₂H₅. In the other three, the key carbon carries two identical groups, so none of them is chiral.","w":["Carbon 2 has two CH₃ groups, so it is not bonded to four different groups.","","The CH carbon bears two CH₃ groups; the OH is on a CH₂, which cannot be chiral.","Carbon 3 has two identical ethyl groups, so it is not chiral despite carrying the OH."]}
{"id":"ch-176","s":"chem","t":"3.3.7","lv":1,"q":"Two enantiomers of the same compound are each dissolved in a solvent and placed in a polarimeter. Which statement is correct?","o":["They rotate the plane by different amounts, both in the same direction","Only one enantiomer rotates the plane; the other has no effect","They have different boiling points, so can be separated by distillation","They rotate the plane by equal amounts but in opposite directions"],"a":3,"x":"Enantiomers are non-superimposable mirror images. They have identical physical properties except that they rotate plane-polarised light by equal amounts in opposite directions. Their boiling points are the same, so distillation cannot separate them.","w":["Enantiomers rotate by equal angles, and in opposite directions; this confuses them with different compounds.","Both enantiomers are optically active; neither is inactive.","Enantiomers have identical boiling points; this wrongly treats them like structural isomers.",""]}
{"id":"ch-177","s":"chem","t":"3.3.7","lv":1,"q":"A racemic mixture of a chiral compound has no effect on plane-polarised light. What is the reason?","o":["It contains equal amounts of two enantiomers, so their rotations cancel","Its molecules have no chiral centre, so they cannot rotate the light","It absorbs the plane-polarised light instead of rotating it","Each molecule has two chiral centres whose effects cancel inside the molecule"],"a":0,"x":"A racemic mixture is a 1:1 mixture of the two enantiomers. Each rotates the plane by the same angle in opposite directions, so the net rotation is zero. The molecules are still chiral; the cancelling is between different molecules.","w":["","A racemic mixture's molecules are chiral; only the mixture is optically inactive.","Polarimetry depends on rotation, not absorption; this invents a wrong mechanism.","The cancelling is between two different enantiomers in the mixture, not within a single molecule."]}
{"id":"ch-178","s":"chem","t":"3.3.7","lv":2,"q":"How many chiral centres are there in one molecule of 3-methylpentan-2-ol, CH₃CH(OH)CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃?","o":["0","1","2","3"],"a":2,"x":"Carbon 2 (H, OH, CH₃, CH(CH₃)C₂H₅) and carbon 3 (H, CH₃, C₂H₅, CH(OH)CH₃) each bond to four different groups. Carbons 1, 4 and 5 and the methyl branch each carry two or more H atoms.","w":["There are carbon atoms with four different groups, so zero is wrong.","This counts only the carbon with the OH group and misses the branched carbon 3.","","Only carbons 2 and 3 are chiral; counting the CH₂ or a CH₃ ignores that they have repeated H atoms."]}
{"id":"ch-179","s":"chem","t":"3.3.7","lv":2,"q":"Which carbonyl compound reacts with KCN and dilute acid to give a racemic mixture of hydroxynitrile enantiomers?","o":["Propanone","Propanal","Methanal","Pentan-3-one"],"a":1,"x":"Propanal gives CH₃CH₂CH(OH)CN, whose central carbon has H, OH, CN and C₂H₅. The planar C=O is attacked equally from either side, giving equal amounts of both enantiomers. The other products have two identical groups on that carbon.","w":["The product has two CH₃ groups on the carbon, so it is achiral and there is no racemic mixture.","","The product HOCH₂CN has two H atoms on that carbon, so it is achiral.","The product has two ethyl groups on that carbon, so it is achiral."]}
{"id":"ch-180","s":"chem","t":"3.3.7","lv":3,"q":"A solution of pure (+) enantiomer gives a rotation of +8.0° in a polarimeter. A mixture of 75% (+) and 25% (−) enantiomer is made up to the same total concentration, in the same tube. What rotation is seen?","o":["+6.0°","+8.0°","0°","+4.0°"],"a":3,"x":"Each enantiomer rotates in proportion to its amount, in opposite directions. The 25% (−) cancels 25% of the (+), leaving a net 50% excess of (+): 0.75 × 8.0 − 0.25 × 8.0 = +4.0°.","w":["This is 75% of 8.0°, ignoring that the (−) enantiomer rotates the light back the other way.","This would be seen only for the pure (+) enantiomer, ignoring the 25% of (−).","Zero applies only to an exactly 50:50 racemic mixture; here the (+) is in excess.",""]}
{"id":"ch-181","s":"chem","t":"3.3.8","lv":1,"q":"Propanal and propanone are each warmed separately with Tollens' reagent. Which observation is correct?","o":["Both compounds give a silver mirror","Propanal gives a silver mirror; propanone shows no change","Propanone gives a silver mirror; propanal shows no change","Propanal gives a brick-red precipitate; propanone shows no change"],"a":1,"x":"Tollens' reagent oxidises aldehydes, reducing Ag⁺ to silver, so propanal gives a mirror. Ketones are not easily oxidised, so propanone gives no change. A brick-red precipitate is the Fehling's result, not Tollens'.","w":["Ketones are not oxidised by Tollens' reagent, so only the aldehyde gives a mirror.","","This is reversed: the aldehyde is oxidised, so it is propanal that gives the mirror.","A brick-red precipitate is the Fehling's test result; Tollens' gives a silver mirror."]}
{"id":"ch-182","s":"chem","t":"3.3.8","lv":1,"q":"Butanone is reduced using NaBH₄ in aqueous solution. Which row gives the organic product and the nucleophile that attacks the carbonyl carbon?","o":["Butan-1-ol; hydride ion, H⁻","Butan-2-ol; hydrogen ion, H⁺","Butanal; hydride ion, H⁻","Butan-2-ol; hydride ion, H⁻"],"a":3,"x":"NaBH₄ is a source of hydride ions, H⁻, which are nucleophiles and attack the δ+ carbonyl carbon. A ketone is reduced to a secondary alcohol, so butanone gives butan-2-ol, not a primary alcohol.","w":["Reducing a ketone gives a secondary alcohol; butan-1-ol would need the OH on the end carbon.","H⁺ is an electrophile and would not attack the δ+ carbonyl carbon; the attacking species is H⁻.","Butanal is an aldehyde isomer, not a reduction product; reduction adds hydrogen and gives an alcohol.",""]}
{"id":"ch-183","s":"chem","t":"3.3.8","lv":2,"q":"Propanal is reacted with KCN followed by dilute sulfuric acid. What is the name of the organic product?","o":["2-hydroxybutanenitrile","2-hydroxypropanenitrile","3-hydroxybutanenitrile","2-hydroxy-2-methylpropanenitrile"],"a":0,"x":"CN⁻ adds to the carbonyl carbon, giving CH₃CH₂CH(OH)CN. The nitrile carbon is C1, so the chain is four carbons with OH on C2. Forgetting to count the CN carbon gives the wrong name.","w":["","This is the product from ethanal; the CN carbon adds one carbon to propanal's three, giving four.","The OH is on the former carbonyl carbon, which is next to the CN carbon, so C2 not C3.","This is the product from propanone, a ketone with two methyl groups, not from propanal."]}
{"id":"ch-184","s":"chem","t":"3.3.8","lv":2,"q":"Ethanal reacts with KCN then dilute acid and the product mixture shows no optical activity. What is the best explanation?","o":["The product has no chiral carbon atom","The product forms by a free radical mechanism","Attack on both faces of the planar C=O gives a racemic mixture","CN⁻ is a symmetrical ion, so only one product can form"],"a":2,"x":"The product CH₃CH(OH)CN has a chiral carbon, but the planar carbonyl group is attacked equally from above and below. This gives a 50:50 racemic mixture whose rotations cancel. The slip is thinking no chiral centre forms.","w":["The product does have a chiral carbon, with four different groups: H, CH₃, OH and CN.","The mechanism is nucleophilic addition by CN⁻, not a free radical mechanism.","","The symmetry of CN⁻ is irrelevant; the product is chiral and both enantiomers form because the carbonyl group is planar."]}
{"id":"ch-185","s":"chem","t":"3.3.8","lv":2,"q":"In the first step of the mechanism for propanone with cyanide ions, where does the curly arrow from the nucleophile start and finish?","o":["From the lone pair on N of CN⁻ to the carbonyl carbon","From the lone pair on C of CN⁻ to the carbonyl carbon","From the C=O double bond to the carbon of CN⁻","From the lone pair on the carbonyl oxygen to the carbon of CN⁻"],"a":1,"x":"The cyanide ion is the nucleophile and uses the lone pair on its carbon to attack the δ+ carbonyl carbon, forming a new C–C bond. The C=O π bond then moves onto oxygen in the same step.","w":["Cyanide attacks using the lone pair on carbon, forming a new C–C bond, not through nitrogen.","","The arrow starts from the nucleophile's lone pair; the C=O arrow goes from the bond onto oxygen, not to CN⁻.","The oxygen is not the nucleophile; the δ+ carbonyl carbon is the site that is attacked."]}
{"id":"ch-186","s":"chem","t":"3.3.8","lv":3,"q":"Compound P, C₅H₁₀O, has an unbranched carbon chain and gives no change with Tollens' reagent. NaBH₄ reduces P to an optically active alcohol. What is P?","o":["Pentan-2-one","Pentan-3-one","Pentanal","3-methylbutan-2-one"],"a":0,"x":"No reaction with Tollens' means a ketone. Unbranched ketones are pentan-2-one and pentan-3-one. Reduction gives pentan-2-ol, which has a chiral carbon, or pentan-3-ol, which has two identical ethyl groups and is not chiral.","w":["","Reduction gives pentan-3-ol, which has two identical ethyl groups on the carbon bearing OH, so it is not chiral.","Pentanal is an aldehyde and would give a silver mirror with Tollens'; it also reduces to a non-chiral primary alcohol.","This ketone does give a chiral alcohol but its chain is branched, which the question rules out."]}
{"id":"ch-187","s":"chem","t":"3.3.9","lv":1,"q":"Propanoic acid is heated under reflux with methanol and a few drops of concentrated sulfuric acid. What is the name of the organic product?","o":["propyl methanoate","methyl ethanoate","methyl propanoate","ethyl propanoate"],"a":2,"x":"Esterification joins the acid's acyl part (propanoate, 3 C) to the alcohol's alkyl group (methyl). This gives methyl propanoate. The trap is swapping which name comes from the acid and which from the alcohol.","w":["Swapped the parts: the name starts with the alcohol's alkyl group and ends with the acid's stem.","Wrong acid chain length: propanoic acid has three carbons, so the ester ends in propanoate, not ethanoate.","","The alkyl group comes from the alcohol; methanol gives methyl, not ethyl."]}
{"id":"ch-188","s":"chem","t":"3.3.9","lv":1,"q":"Which test gives a positive result with ethanoic acid but NOT with ethanol?","o":["Warm with acidified potassium dichromate(VI): orange to green","Add sodium hydrogencarbonate: effervescence, gas turns limewater milky","Add bromine water: orange solution decolourised","Warm with Tollens' reagent: silver mirror forms"],"a":1,"x":"Carboxylic acids are acidic enough to react with hydrogencarbonates, releasing CO₂. Ethanol does not. Dichromate is the trap: it oxidises ethanol, not ethanoic acid, so it is positive for the alcohol.","w":["Ethanol is oxidised and turns the dichromate green; ethanoic acid does not, so this is the reverse.","","Neither compound has a C=C bond, so neither decolourises bromine water.","Tollens' reagent detects aldehydes; neither ethanoic acid nor ethanol gives a silver mirror."]}
{"id":"ch-189","s":"chem","t":"3.3.9","lv":2,"q":"Biodiesel is made by heating a vegetable oil (a triglyceride) with methanol and a base catalyst. How many moles of methanol react per mole of triglyceride, and what is the other product?","o":["1 mol; propane-1,2,3-triol","3 mol; propanoic acid","3 mol; water","3 mol; propane-1,2,3-triol"],"a":3,"x":"Each of the three ester groups swaps its glycerol-derived part for a methyl group, forming 3 methyl esters (biodiesel) and propane-1,2,3-triol (glycerol). The trap is treating it as hydrolysis or esterification that makes water.","w":["Only one ester link is converted; a triglyceride has three ester groups, so 3 mol methanol are needed.","Propanoic acid is not formed; the glycerol backbone is released as the alcohol propane-1,2,3-triol.","Water forms in esterification from an acid; this is transesterification, so no water is produced.",""]}
{"id":"ch-190","s":"chem","t":"3.3.9","lv":2,"q":"Propanoyl chloride reacts with ethylamine (CH₃CH₂NH₂) in a nucleophilic addition–elimination reaction. What is the organic product?","o":["N-ethylpropanamide","N-propylethanamide","ethyl propanoate","propanamide"],"a":0,"x":"The amine's nitrogen attacks the acyl carbon and Cl is lost as HCl, giving CH₃CH₂CONHCH₂CH₃, N-ethylpropanamide. The trap is mixing up which chain is on the carbonyl and which is on nitrogen.","w":["","Chains swapped: the propanoyl group stays on the carbonyl carbon, the ethyl group is on nitrogen.","This ester would form with ethanol, not ethylamine; the amine gives an N-substituted amide.","Propanamide would form with ammonia; ethylamine keeps its ethyl group on the nitrogen."]}
{"id":"ch-191","s":"chem","t":"3.3.9","lv":2,"q":"A student recrystallises crude aspirin (data book melting point 135 °C). Which result for the dry crystals best shows they are pure?","o":["Melts over an 8 °C range that ends at 135 °C","Melts sharply at 128 °C, 7 °C below the data value","Melts at 142 °C, above the data value","Melts over a 1 °C range at about 135 °C"],"a":3,"x":"A pure solid melts over a very narrow range at its data book value. Impurities lower the melting point and broaden the range. They never raise it, so a higher value is not a sign of purity.","w":["A wide range is typical of an impure sample, even if it ends at the correct value.","A value well below the data figure indicates impurities or a different compound; it is not a match.","Impurities lower rather than raise a melting point, so a higher value does not show purity.",""]}
{"id":"ch-192","s":"chem","t":"3.3.9","lv":3,"q":"2.00 g of salicylic acid, C₇H₆O₃, reacts with excess ethanoic anhydride (1:1 mole ratio) to give 2.16 g of pure aspirin, C₉H₈O₄. Ar: C 12, H 1, O 16. What is the percentage yield?","o":["82.8%","108%","141%","41.4%"],"a":0,"x":"Mr salicylic acid = 138, aspirin = 180. Moles = 2.00 ÷ 138 = 0.01449, so theoretical mass = 0.01449 × 180 = 2.61 g. Yield = 2.16 ÷ 2.61 × 100 = 82.8%. Ignoring the differing Mr values is the trap.","w":["","Compared the masses directly (2.16 ÷ 2.00), ignoring that aspirin and salicylic acid have different molar masses.","Inverted the Mr ratio (138 ÷ 180), giving a theoretical mass too small; a yield over 100% should flag the error.","Doubled the theoretical yield by assuming 2 mol aspirin per mole; the ratio is 1:1."]}
{"id":"ch-193","s":"chem","t":"3.3.10","lv":1,"q":"Which observation about benzene supports the delocalised model rather than Kekulé's structure with alternating single and double bonds?","o":["Its molecular formula is C₆H₆","It burns with a smoky flame","All its carbon–carbon bonds are the same length, between C–C and C=C","It has a boiling point of about 80 °C"],"a":2,"x":"Kekulé's ring would have three long C–C and three short C=C bonds. Equal bond lengths (0.139 nm, between 0.154 and 0.134 nm) show the π electrons are spread evenly round the ring.","w":["The formula fits both models, so it cannot tell them apart.","A smoky flame only shows a high carbon to hydrogen ratio, not anything about the bonding.","","Boiling point reflects intermolecular forces and does not show whether the bonds are delocalised."]}
{"id":"ch-194","s":"chem","t":"3.3.10","lv":2,"q":"ΔH(hydrogenation) is −120 kJ mol⁻¹ for cyclohexene and −208 kJ mol⁻¹ for benzene. By how much is benzene more stable than hypothetical cyclohexa-1,3,5-triene?","o":["32 kJ mol⁻¹","88 kJ mol⁻¹","328 kJ mol⁻¹","152 kJ mol⁻¹"],"a":3,"x":"Kekulé's triene would release 3 × (−120) = −360 kJ mol⁻¹. Actual is −208, so benzene is 360 − 208 = 152 kJ mol⁻¹ more stable. The trap is using one or two C=C bonds instead of three.","w":["Used two C=C bonds (240 − 208); benzene has three double bonds in the Kekulé model.","Subtracted one cyclohexene value from the benzene value (208 − 120), not the predicted triene value.","Added 120 and 208 instead of finding the difference between predicted and actual.",""]}
{"id":"ch-195","s":"chem","t":"3.3.10","lv":1,"q":"Benzene is nitrated using concentrated nitric and sulfuric acids. Which statement correctly gives the electrophile and how it forms?","o":["NO₂⁺, formed when HNO₃ accepts a proton from H₂SO₄ then loses H₂O","NO₂⁺, formed when HNO₃ donates a proton to H₂SO₄ acting as a base","NO₃⁻, formed when HNO₃ donates a proton to H₂SO₄","NO₂⁻, formed when concentrated H₂SO₄ reduces HNO₃"],"a":0,"x":"The stronger acid H₂SO₄ protonates HNO₃, which loses water to give the nitronium ion NO₂⁺. The trap is reversing the acid–base roles or choosing a negative ion, which cannot attack an electron-rich ring.","w":["","Roles reversed: H₂SO₄ is the stronger acid, so HNO₃ acts as the base here.","NO₃⁻ is an anion, so it would be repelled by the π electrons and is not the electrophile.","Sulfuric acid is not a reducing agent here, and NO₂⁻ is a nucleophile, not an electrophile."]}
{"id":"ch-196","s":"chem","t":"3.3.10","lv":2,"q":"Benzene reacts with propanoyl chloride, CH₃CH₂COCl, in the presence of anhydrous AlCl₃. What is the organic product?","o":["Propylbenzene","Phenyl propanoate","(1-Methylethyl)benzene","1-Phenylpropan-1-one"],"a":3,"x":"Friedel–Crafts acylation: the acylium ion CH₃CH₂CO⁺ substitutes an H on the ring, giving C₆H₅COCH₂CH₃, a ketone. The C=O is retained, so it is not an alkylbenzene or an ester.","w":["That is an alkylbenzene with no C=O; the acyl group keeps its C=O.","An ester needs a C–O–C link to the ring; the acylium ion bonds through carbon, not oxygen.","This is a branched alkylbenzene with no C=O; the acyl group keeps its C=O.",""]}
{"id":"ch-197","s":"chem","t":"3.3.10","lv":2,"q":"Benzene reacts with CH₃COCl and AlCl₃ to give phenylethanone. Which statement describes the final step of the mechanism?","o":["The intermediate loses H⁺, restoring the ring, and H⁺ reacts with AlCl₄⁻ to regenerate AlCl₃ and form HCl","The intermediate gains Cl⁻ to give an addition product, and AlCl₃ is used up","The intermediate loses H⁺, which combines with AlCl₃ to give HAlCl₃, so AlCl₃ is used up","The intermediate loses CH₃CO⁺, which reforms CH₃COCl and AlCl₃"],"a":0,"x":"The positive intermediate loses H⁺, its electrons return to the ring, and aromatic stability is restored. H⁺ combines with AlCl₄⁻ to give HCl and regenerate AlCl₃, so AlCl₃ is a catalyst.","w":["","Addition would destroy the stable delocalised ring; substitution restores it, and AlCl₃ is regenerated, not consumed.","H⁺ reacts with AlCl₄⁻ (formed in the first step), not AlCl₃; the catalyst is regenerated.","Losing CH₃CO⁺ would just reverse the attack step and give no substitution product."]}
{"id":"ch-198","s":"chem","t":"3.3.10","lv":2,"q":"7.80 g of benzene (M = 78) is nitrated to nitrobenzene, C₆H₅NO₂ (M = 123), with a 60% yield. What mass of nitrobenzene is obtained?","o":["12.3 g","7.38 g","4.68 g","20.5 g"],"a":1,"x":"Moles of benzene = 7.80 ÷ 78 = 0.100 mol, giving 0.100 mol nitrobenzene theoretically (1:1). Theoretical mass = 12.3 g; at 60% this is 12.3 × 0.60 = 7.38 g. Traps: forgetting the yield or using benzene's mass.","w":["This is the theoretical mass; the 60% yield has not been applied.","","Applied 60% to the benzene mass (7.80 × 0.6), ignoring the different molar mass of the product.","Divided by 0.60 instead of multiplying, so the 'actual' yield exceeds the theoretical maximum."]}
{"id":"ch-199","s":"chem","t":"3.3.11","lv":1,"q":"Ethylamine is to be made from bromoethane and ammonia with as little further substitution as possible. Which conditions are best?","o":["Excess bromoethane with a little ammonia in aqueous solution","A large excess of ammonia in ethanol, heated in a sealed tube","Equimolar bromoethane and ammonia, heated under reflux in water","Excess bromoethane with ethylamine in ethanol, left cold"],"a":1,"x":"Ethylamine is itself a nucleophile and can attack more bromoethane. A large excess of ammonia makes bromoethane far more likely to meet NH₃ than the product. The sealed tube keeps volatile ammonia in, and ethanol avoids hydrolysis by water.","w":["Excess bromoethane makes further substitution more likely, giving secondary and tertiary amines and quaternary salts.","","Equimolar amounts still give a mixture because the amine product competes with ammonia as a nucleophile.","Excess bromoethane with the amine would give diethylamine, triethylamine and quaternary salts, not ethylamine."]}
{"id":"ch-200","s":"chem","t":"3.3.11","lv":1,"q":"Propanenitrile, CH₃CH₂CN, is converted into propylamine, CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂. Which reagent and conditions achieve this?","o":["Reflux with dilute hydrochloric acid","Heat with ammonia in ethanol","Acidified potassium dichromate(VI), heated","LiAlH₄ in dry ether"],"a":3,"x":"The C≡N group must be reduced by adding hydrogen to make CH₂NH₂. LiAlH₄ in dry ether (or H₂ with a nickel catalyst) does this. Acid hydrolysis changes the nitrile to a carboxylic acid instead.","w":["Dilute acid hydrolyses the nitrile to propanoic acid; this is hydrolysis, not reduction.","Ammonia is a nucleophile, not a reducing agent, so it cannot add hydrogen to the nitrile.","Dichromate is an oxidising agent; it removes hydrogen rather than adding it to the C≡N group.",""]}
{"id":"ch-201","s":"chem","t":"3.3.11","lv":2,"q":"Which shows the correct order of increasing basicity of phenylamine, ammonia and ethylamine?","o":["phenylamine < ammonia < ethylamine","ethylamine < ammonia < phenylamine","ammonia < phenylamine < ethylamine","ammonia < ethylamine < phenylamine"],"a":0,"x":"Basicity depends on how available the nitrogen lone pair is. The ethyl group pushes electron density onto N, making it most basic. In phenylamine the lone pair is delocalised into the ring, making it least basic.","w":["","Reversed: the alkyl group is electron-donating and raises basicity, so ethylamine is the strongest base, not the weakest.","Delocalisation of the lone pair into the benzene ring makes phenylamine weaker than ammonia, not stronger.","Phenylamine is placed as strongest, but delocalisation of its lone pair into the ring makes it the weakest base."]}
{"id":"ch-202","s":"chem","t":"3.3.11","lv":2,"q":"Methylamine reacts with ethanoyl chloride by nucleophilic addition–elimination. What is the organic product?","o":["CH₃CONH₂, ethanamide","CH₃COOCH₃, methyl ethanoate","CH₃CONHCH₃, N-methylethanamide","CH₃CON(CH₃)₂, N,N-dimethylethanamide"],"a":2,"x":"The nitrogen lone pair attacks the C=O carbon, then Cl⁻ is lost with H⁺ from nitrogen. The product is an N-substituted amide, CH₃CONHCH₃, and the HCl formed reacts with more methylamine.","w":["Ethanamide would need ammonia as the nucleophile; here the methyl group on nitrogen is kept.","An ester would need methanol; the nucleophile here is the nitrogen of the amine, not oxygen.","","This needs two methyl groups on nitrogen, which would require dimethylamine, not methylamine."]}
{"id":"ch-203","s":"chem","t":"3.3.11","lv":2,"q":"Nitrobenzene is reduced by heating with tin and concentrated HCl. Excess NaOH is then added. What is the purpose of the NaOH?","o":["To reduce any remaining nitrobenzene to phenylamine","To deprotonate C₆H₅NH₃⁺ and release free phenylamine","To protonate the nitrogen so phenylamine dissolves in water","To hydrolyse phenylamine to phenol"],"a":1,"x":"In the acid the product exists as the phenylammonium salt, C₆H₅NH₃⁺. Excess alkali removes H⁺ from the nitrogen, liberating the free base C₆H₅NH₂, which can then be separated.","w":["NaOH is not a reducing agent; the reduction was carried out by tin and HCl.","","NaOH is a base and removes protons rather than adding them; protonation would form the salt.","Amines are not hydrolysed to phenols by NaOH under these conditions; the NaOH simply neutralises the acid and the salt."]}
{"id":"ch-204","s":"chem","t":"3.3.11","lv":3,"q":"0.020 mol of ethylamine is heated with excess bromoethane until fully alkylated. Assuming 100% yield, what is the maximum mass of the ionic product? (Ar: C 12.0, H 1.0, N 14.0, Br 79.9)","o":["4.2 g","3.6 g","2.0 g","3.1 g"],"a":0,"x":"Excess bromoethane keeps substituting until the quaternary salt (C₂H₅)₄N⁺Br⁻ forms, Mr 209.9. Mass = 0.020 × 209.9 ≈ 4.2 g. Stopping at an earlier stage gives a lower mass.","w":["","This is the mass of triethylammonium bromide (Mr 182), which stops one step short of the quaternary salt.","This is triethylamine (Mr 101), a neutral tertiary amine; excess bromoethane would quaternise it further.","This is diethylammonium bromide (Mr 154), an early stage that would react further with excess bromoethane."]}
{"id":"ch-205","s":"chem","t":"3.3.12","lv":1,"q":"Which statement correctly describes the formation of a polyester?","o":["A diol reacts with a dicarboxylic acid, and a water molecule is lost at each new ester link","A diamine reacts with a dicarboxylic acid, and a water molecule is lost at each new ester link","A diol reacts with a dicarboxylic acid, and the C=C bonds open so that nothing is lost","A diol reacts with a dicarboxylic acid, and a hydrogen molecule is lost at each new link"],"a":0,"x":"Polyesters form by condensation polymerisation. The –OH groups of the diol react with the –COOH groups of the diacid, forming ester links and losing one H₂O per link. No C=C bonds are involved.","w":["","A diamine and a diacid make a polyamide, which has amide links, not ester links.","That describes addition polymerisation; condensation polymers lose a small molecule and need no C=C.","The small molecule lost is water, not hydrogen; the –OH and –H atoms leave together as H₂O."]}
{"id":"ch-206","s":"chem","t":"3.3.12","lv":2,"q":"6-aminohexanoic acid, H₂N(CH₂)₅COOH, polymerises to give a polyamide. How many water molecules are eliminated when 100 monomer molecules join to form one chain?","o":["100","50","99","198"],"a":2,"x":"Each amide link joins two monomers and loses one H₂O. A chain of 100 monomers has 99 links, so 99 water molecules are lost. The trap is assuming one water per monomer.","w":["Assumes one water per monomer; the number of links is one fewer than the number of monomers.","Treats monomers as joining in pairs; every monomer joins to the next, so there are 99 links.","","Counts two waters per link; only one H₂O is lost per amide link formed."]}
{"id":"ch-207","s":"chem","t":"3.3.12","lv":2,"q":"Nylon-6,6 forms from hexanedioic acid (Mr 146) and hexane-1,6-diamine (Mr 116). What is the relative mass of one repeat unit, C₁₂H₂₂N₂O₂?","o":["262","226","244","208"],"a":1,"x":"One repeat unit comes from one diacid and one diamine joined by two amide links. Two H₂O (36) are lost: 146 + 116 − 36 = 226. The trap is losing only one water, or none.","w":["This is 146 + 116 with no water lost; condensation polymerisation eliminates H₂O.","","This subtracts only one H₂O (18); a repeat unit contains two amide links, so two H₂O are lost.","This subtracts three H₂O (54); only two links per repeat unit means two waters lost."]}
{"id":"ch-208","s":"chem","t":"3.3.12","lv":1,"q":"Why can polyesters be broken down by hydrolysis, whereas poly(propene) is not biodegradable?","o":["Polyesters contain C=C bonds that micro-organisms can break","Polyesters have a higher Mr, so they break down more easily","Polyesters have hydrogen bonds that let them dissolve in water","Polyesters have polar ester links; poly(propene) has only non-polar bonds"],"a":3,"x":"The ester carbon is δ+ because of the polar C=O and C–O bonds, so water or enzymes can attack it and hydrolyse the chain. Poly(propene) has non-polar C–C and C–H bonds and resists attack.","w":["Polyester chains have no C=C bonds; their links are ester groups formed by condensation.","Chain length does not decide biodegradability; polar groups open to nucleophilic attack do.","Intermolecular forces do not make polyesters dissolve; the key point is hydrolysis at polar ester links.",""]}
{"id":"ch-209","s":"chem","t":"3.3.12","lv":3,"q":"A polyamide has repeat unit –CO(CH₂)₄CONH(CH₂)₂NH–. It is hydrolysed by heating with excess dilute hydrochloric acid. Which products are formed?","o":["Hexanedioic acid and ethane-1,2-diammonium chloride","Hexanedioic acid and ethane-1,2-diamine","Hexane-1,6-diol and ethane-1,2-diamine","Sodium hexanedioate and ethane-1,2-diamine"],"a":0,"x":"Acid hydrolysis breaks the amide links to give the dicarboxylic acid (6 C) and the diamine. Excess HCl protonates the amine groups, so the diamine is present as its ammonium chloride salt.","w":["","Ignores the excess HCl; amines are basic and would be protonated to –NH₃⁺ Cl⁻ in acid.","Amide hydrolysis gives a carboxylic acid, not an alcohol; the diol would come from a polyester.","This would form under alkaline (NaOH) conditions, not with hydrochloric acid."]}
{"id":"ch-210","s":"chem","t":"3.3.12","lv":1,"q":"Which statement about disposing of waste polymers is correct?","o":["All polymers biodegrade in landfill, so landfill is the best disposal method","Feedstock recycling turns polymers into monomers or fuels with no energy input","Burning them can generate electricity, but toxic HCl from PVC must be removed","Melting and remoulding waste polymers always gives products of identical quality"],"a":2,"x":"Incineration with energy recovery uses the polymer's energy content, but burning halogenated polymers such as PVC gives toxic HCl, which must be removed by scrubbing. Many polymers do not biodegrade.","w":["Many polymers, such as addition polymers, are not biodegradable and persist in landfill.","Feedstock recycling needs energy for cracking or hydrolysis; it is not energy-free.","","Remoulding often degrades quality and needs sorted polymers, so quality is not always the same."]}
{"id":"ch-211","s":"chem","t":"3.3.13","lv":2,"q":"Alanine, CH₃CH(NH₂)COOH, is dissolved in a strongly acidic solution (pH 1). Which species is present in the greatest amount?","o":["CH₃CH(NH₂)COO⁻","CH₃CH(NH₃⁺)COOH","CH₃CH(NH₃⁺)COO⁻","CH₃CH(NH₂)COOH"],"a":1,"x":"In strong acid the basic –NH₂ group is protonated to –NH₃⁺, and the –COOH group stays protonated. The result is a cation. The zwitterion forms only near neutral pH, and the trap is choosing it for every solution.","w":["This is the anion that forms in alkaline solution, where –COOH loses H⁺ and –NH₂ stays unprotonated.","","This is the zwitterion, found near neutral pH; in strong acid the –COO⁻ group would gain H⁺.","The amine group is basic and would accept H⁺ at pH 1, so the uncharged form does not predominate."]}
{"id":"ch-212","s":"chem","t":"3.3.13","lv":2,"q":"A linear polypeptide is made from 50 amino acid residues joined by peptide links. How many water molecules are needed to hydrolyse one molecule completely into amino acids?","o":["25","48","50","49"],"a":3,"x":"Joining n amino acids in a chain forms n − 1 peptide links, each made by condensation with loss of one water. Complete hydrolysis therefore needs n − 1 = 49 water molecules. The trap is using n.","w":["This halves the number of residues; each peptide link needs one water, and there are not 25 links.","This subtracts 2 instead of 1; a linear chain of 50 residues has 49 links, not 48.","This assumes one water per residue, but a linear chain has one fewer peptide link than residues.",""]}
{"id":"ch-213","s":"chem","t":"3.3.13","lv":1,"q":"Which interaction holds a protein chain in the α-helix, which is part of its secondary structure?","o":["Hydrogen bonds between the N–H and C=O groups of the peptide backbone","Disulfide bridges between the R groups of cysteine residues","Ionic interactions between oppositely charged R groups","Covalent peptide links between neighbouring amino acid residues"],"a":0,"x":"The α-helix is held by hydrogen bonds between N–H and C=O groups in the backbone, four residues apart. Interactions between R groups, such as disulfide bridges and ionic bonds, are tertiary structure. Peptide links are primary structure.","w":["","Disulfide bridges involve R groups and help hold the tertiary structure, not the secondary structure.","Ionic interactions between R groups are part of tertiary structure, not the backbone hydrogen bonding of the helix.","Peptide links make up the primary structure; they join the chain but do not coil it into a helix."]}
{"id":"ch-214","s":"chem","t":"3.3.13","lv":2,"q":"An enzyme catalyses a reaction of only one enantiomer of a chiral substrate and does not act on its mirror image. What is the best explanation?","o":["The other enantiomer has different functional groups, so cannot be hydrolysed","The other enantiomer has a different molecular formula, so cannot enter the active site","Only one enantiomer has the 3D shape that fits the chiral active site","The two enantiomers have different melting points, so only one dissolves in the cell"],"a":2,"x":"An active site is a specific three-dimensional shape, made of chiral amino acids. Only one enantiomer fits and binds, so the enzyme is stereospecific. The trap is thinking enantiomers differ in formula or groups; they don't.","w":["Enantiomers have identical functional groups and the same bonding; they differ only in 3D arrangement.","Enantiomers have the same molecular formula; only their spatial arrangement differs.","","Enantiomers have identical physical properties such as melting point and solubility in an achiral solvent."]}
{"id":"ch-215","s":"chem","t":"3.3.13","lv":2,"q":"Cisplatin, Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂, is used as an anticancer drug. Which statement describes how it acts on DNA?","o":["The NH₃ ligands are replaced by DNA bases, forming bonds that stop cell division","Both Cl⁻ ligands are replaced by guanine N atoms, forming coordinate bonds","It hydrogen bonds to phosphate groups, cutting the sugar–phosphate backbone","Pt²⁺ forms ionic bonds with the sugar–phosphate backbone, stopping transcription"],"a":1,"x":"The two chloride ligands are replaced by ligand substitution. Nitrogen atoms on guanine bases donate lone pairs to platinum, forming coordinate bonds. This distorts the DNA so it cannot replicate. The trap is thinking the ammonia ligands are replaced; they stay.","w":["The NH₃ ligands remain bound; it is the chloride ligands that are substituted by the DNA bases.","","Cisplatin does not cut the backbone; it binds to nitrogen atoms of guanine bases through coordinate bonds.","Cisplatin is a neutral complex that bonds to bases by coordinate bonds, not ionic bonds to the backbone."]}
{"id":"ch-216","s":"chem","t":"3.3.13","lv":3,"q":"One strand of DNA has the base sequence TTAGCCGC. How many hydrogen bonds form between this strand and its complementary strand? (A–T has two, G–C has three.)","o":["16","19","21","24"],"a":2,"x":"The strand has 3 bases of A or T and 5 of G or C, so there are 3 A–T pairs and 5 G–C pairs. The total is 3 × 2 + 5 × 3 = 6 + 15 = 21 hydrogen bonds.","w":["This gives every base pair two hydrogen bonds, forgetting that G–C pairs have three.","This swaps the numbers, giving A–T three bonds and G–C two: 9 + 10 = 19.","","This gives every base pair three hydrogen bonds, forgetting that A–T pairs have only two."]}
{"id":"ch-217","s":"chem","t":"3.3.14","lv":1,"q":"Why do chemists usually prefer a synthesis route with fewer steps?","o":["Fewer steps guarantee that every step has a 100% atom economy","Each step loses some product, so overall yield falls and more waste is made","Each extra step makes the intermediates more hazardous to handle","Fewer steps always give a purer final product with no purification needed"],"a":1,"x":"Yields below 100% multiply, so each extra step cuts the overall yield. More steps also need more reagents, energy, time and waste disposal. Atom economy depends on each equation, not on the number of steps.","w":["Atom economy depends on the equation for each reaction, not on how many steps there are.","","Intermediates are not always more hazardous. The real problem is lost yield and extra resources.","Fewer steps may still need purification, and purity is not the main reason."]}
{"id":"ch-218","s":"chem","t":"3.3.14","lv":1,"q":"Which type of reaction can have an atom economy of 100% for the desired product?","o":["Elimination of HBr from a bromoalkane","Nucleophilic substitution of a bromoalkane by OH⁻","Esterification of an alcohol with a carboxylic acid","Addition of HBr to an alkene"],"a":3,"x":"In addition, all reactant atoms end up in the single product, so atom economy is 100%. Elimination, substitution and condensation reactions all form a second product, which is waste.","w":["Elimination produces a small molecule (HBr) as well as the alkene, so atoms are wasted.","Substitution produces a bromide salt as a by-product, so atom economy is below 100%.","Esterification also produces water, so not all reactant atoms are in the ester.",""]}
{"id":"ch-219","s":"chem","t":"3.3.14","lv":2,"q":"A three-step synthesis has step yields of 80%, 75% and 50%. What is the overall percentage yield?","o":["30%","68%","50%","3.0%"],"a":0,"x":"Multiply the fractional yields: 0.80 × 0.75 × 0.50 = 0.30, so 30%. Averaging the yields or choosing the lowest one is the trap; each step works on what the previous step produced.","w":["","This is the mean of the three yields; they must be multiplied because each step starts from the previous product.","This is just the lowest step yield; the earlier steps also lose product.","This is a decimal slip: 0.30 was converted to 3.0% instead of 30%."]}
{"id":"ch-220","s":"chem","t":"3.3.14","lv":2,"q":"Which route converts bromoethane, CH₃CH₂Br, into propylamine, CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂?","o":["Excess NH₃ in ethanol, heated in a sealed tube","KCN in ethanol, reflux; then dilute HCl, reflux","KCN in ethanol, reflux; then LiAlH₄ in dry ether","NaOH(aq), reflux; then NH₃ in ethanol"],"a":2,"x":"Propylamine has one more carbon than bromoethane, so a nitrile step is needed. KCN gives CH₃CH₂CN, and reducing it with LiAlH₄ gives CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂. Hydrolysing the nitrile gives an acid instead.","w":["This gives ethylamine, which has only two carbons; the carbon chain is not extended.","Acid hydrolysis of the nitrile gives propanoic acid, not an amine; reduction is needed.","","NaOH(aq) gives ethanol, which does not react with NH₃ to give an amine, and no carbon is added."]}
{"id":"ch-221","s":"chem","t":"3.3.14","lv":2,"q":"Fermentation: C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂. Using Mr values of 180 for glucose, 46 for ethanol and 44 for CO₂, what is the atom economy for ethanol?","o":["25.6%","51.1%","66.7%","48.9%"],"a":1,"x":"Atom economy = (2 × 46) ÷ 180 × 100 = 51.1%. Forgetting to multiply the ethanol Mr by its coefficient of 2 gives 25.6%. CO₂ is the waste product.","w":["This uses 46 instead of 2 × 46, forgetting the coefficient of 2 in the equation.","","This counts carbon atoms only (4 of 6), but atom economy uses relative formula masses.","This is the percentage of the mass that ends up as CO₂ waste, not as ethanol."]}
{"id":"ch-222","s":"chem","t":"3.3.14","lv":3,"q":"Route A has two steps with yields of 90% and 60%. Route B has three steps, each with an 85% yield. Which statement is correct?","o":["A is 75% overall and B is 85%, so B gives more product","A is 60% overall and B is 85%, so B gives more product","A is 54% overall and B is 61%, so B gives more product despite more steps","A is 54% overall and B is 72%, so B gives more product"],"a":2,"x":"Multiply yields: A = 0.90 × 0.60 = 0.54; B = 0.85³ = 0.614. B gives more despite an extra step, so fewer steps does not always mean a higher yield. Averaging or ignoring steps are the traps.","w":["These are mean yields per step; overall yield needs the yields multiplied.","This takes only the last step's yield and ignores the losses in earlier steps.","","0.85² = 0.72 counts only two steps for B; it has three, so 0.85³ = 0.61."]}
{"id":"ch-223","s":"chem","t":"3.3.15","lv":1,"q":"How many peaks would you expect in the carbon-13 NMR spectrum of 2-methylbutane, (CH₃)₂CHCH₂CH₃?","o":["5","4","3","2"],"a":1,"x":"The two CH₃ groups on C2 are equivalent, so the five carbons give four environments: 2 × CH₃, CH, CH₂ and the end CH₃. Counting every carbon (5) ignores the symmetry.","w":["Counted every carbon and ignored that the two CH₃ groups on the CH carbon are equivalent.","","Grouped carbons by type (CH₃, CH₂, CH) instead of environment; the two different CH₃ groups give separate peaks.","Too few: the CH, CH₂ and the two kinds of CH₃ are all in different environments."]}
{"id":"ch-224","s":"chem","t":"3.3.15","lv":1,"q":"Why is tetramethylsilane (TMS) used as the reference standard in proton NMR?","o":["It dissolves the sample and gives no signal of its own","It removes spin–spin splitting from the other peaks","Its twelve equivalent H atoms give one sharp peak at δ = 0, away from most other peaks","Its peak has the highest δ, so every other peak has a negative shift"],"a":2,"x":"TMS has twelve equivalent hydrogens, giving a single strong peak at δ = 0 that is outside the range of most other protons. It is also inert. Solvent role belongs to deuterated solvents, not TMS.","w":["Confused TMS with a deuterated solvent; TMS has H atoms and does give a signal, which is the reference.","Splitting comes from neighbouring protons and TMS does not affect it.","","TMS is at δ = 0, the low end; most other protons have positive shifts."]}
{"id":"ch-225","s":"chem","t":"3.3.15","lv":2,"q":"Ethyl ethanoate, CH₃COOCH₂CH₃, has a ¹H NMR peak at about δ 4.1 from the OCH₂ protons. What are its splitting pattern and relative peak area?","o":["Quartet, relative area 2","Triplet, relative area 2","Quartet, relative area 3","Singlet, relative area 2"],"a":0,"x":"The CH₂ has three H on the adjacent CH₃, so n + 1 = 4 gives a quartet. Peak area is the number of H in that environment: 2. Splitting number and area are different things.","w":["","A triplet needs two neighbouring H; the CH₂ is next to a CH₃ with three, so n + 1 = 4.","Mixed up splitting with area; area equals the 2 H in the CH₂, not the 3 neighbouring H.","Assumed the ester oxygen isolates the CH₂, but the adjacent CH₃ still splits it."]}
{"id":"ch-226","s":"chem","t":"3.3.15","lv":2,"q":"A compound C₅H₁₀O has a carbon-13 peak at δ 210 and only 3 carbon-13 peaks in total. Its ¹H NMR spectrum shows only a triplet and a quartet. What is it?","o":["Pentan-2-one","3-Methylbutan-2-one","Pentanal","Pentan-3-one"],"a":3,"x":"Pentan-3-one, (CH₃CH₂)₂C=O, is symmetrical: three carbon environments, with C=O near 210, and its ¹H spectrum is just CH₃ triplet and CH₂ quartet. Pentan-2-one has five carbon environments.","w":["Has five different carbons and a CH₃ singlet next to C=O, so it gives five carbon peaks and extra signals.","Gives four carbon peaks and a singlet, septet and doublet in ¹H NMR, not a triplet and quartet.","An aldehyde has five carbon environments and a ¹H peak near δ 9.8, so it fails both clues.",""]}
{"id":"ch-228","s":"chem","t":"3.3.15","lv":3,"q":"A compound C₄H₈O₂ has ¹H NMR peaks: δ 1.1 triplet (3H), δ 2.3 quartet (2H), δ 3.7 singlet (3H). Which compound fits?","o":["Ethyl ethanoate","Butanoic acid","Methyl propanoate","Propyl methanoate"],"a":2,"x":"Triplet plus quartet means CH₃CH₂, with the CH₂ at δ 2.3 next to C=O. The 3H singlet at δ 3.7 is OCH₃. This is CH₃CH₂COOCH₃. Ethyl ethanoate would show its quartet near δ 4.1.","w":["Its OCH₂ quartet would be near δ 4.1 and its CH₃CO singlet near δ 2.0, so the shifts do not match.","A carboxylic acid would show a 1H peak near δ 11 and no 3H singlet at δ 3.7.","","It would show a 1H singlet near δ 8 and a 6-proton pattern including a sextet; no 3H singlet."]}
{"id":"ch-229","s":"chem","t":"3.3.16","lv":1,"q":"In thin-layer chromatography of a mixture of amino acids on a silica-coated plate, what are the stationary phase and the mobile phase?","o":["Stationary: the solvent; mobile: the silica layer on the plate","Stationary: the baseline spot; mobile: the solvent front","Stationary: the silica layer on the plate; mobile: the solvent rising up it","Stationary: the filter paper; mobile: the locating agent"],"a":2,"x":"The solid silica layer stays fixed, so it is the stationary phase. The solvent moves up the plate carrying the components, so it is the mobile phase. Separation depends on each component's relative attraction to the two phases.","w":["This swaps the phases: the solvent moves, so it is mobile, and the silica is fixed.","The spot and the solvent front are positions on the plate, not phases.","","The locating agent only makes spots visible afterwards, and the silica plate is the stationary phase."]}
{"id":"ch-230","s":"chem","t":"3.3.16","lv":2,"q":"A mixture of pentane and heptane is analysed by gas chromatography with helium as carrier gas and a non-polar liquid stationary phase. Which has the longer retention time, and why?","o":["Heptane, because its stronger London forces with the stationary phase hold it back longer","Pentane, because it is more volatile so it interacts with the stationary phase for longer","Heptane, because its greater mass means helium carries it through the column faster","Neither, because both alkanes have the same functional group"],"a":0,"x":"Heptane has more electrons, so stronger London forces with the stationary phase. It spends more time in the stationary phase and less in the gas, so it emerges later. The trap is thinking same functional group means same time.","w":["","More volatile molecules spend more time in the gas phase, so pentane leaves first, not last.","Retention depends on attraction to the stationary phase; heptane is held back more, so it emerges later.","Chain length changes the strength of London forces, so the two alkanes have different retention times."]}
{"id":"ch-231","s":"chem","t":"3.3.16","lv":2,"q":"A mixture of hexane and hexan-1-ol is passed through a column of polar alumina using a non-polar solvent. Which compound is eluted first, and why?","o":["Hexan-1-ol, because hydrogen bonding to the alumina carries it down faster","Hexan-1-ol, because its larger Mr makes it move through the column faster","Both together, because they have the same carbon chain length","Hexane, because it has weaker attraction to the polar alumina than the alcohol"],"a":3,"x":"Hexan-1-ol can hydrogen bond to the polar alumina, so it is adsorbed more strongly and moves more slowly. Hexane has only weak London forces with alumina, so it is eluted first. The trap is thinking stronger attraction means faster movement.","w":["Hydrogen bonding to the stationary phase holds the alcohol back, so it moves slower, not faster.","Mr does not control the order of elution here. Attraction to the polar stationary phase does.","The OH group changes polarity, so the compounds interact differently with alumina and separate.",""]}
{"id":"ch-232","s":"chem","t":"3.3.16","lv":3,"q":"On a TLC plate the pencil baseline is 1.0 cm above the bottom edge. The centre of a spot is 4.2 cm above the bottom edge and the solvent front reached 7.0 cm. What is its Rf value?","o":["0.60","0.53","0.46","0.70"],"a":1,"x":"Distances must be measured from the baseline. The spot moved 4.2 − 1.0 = 3.2 cm and the solvent moved 7.0 − 1.0 = 6.0 cm. Rf = 3.2 ÷ 6.0 = 0.53. The trap is measuring from the bottom edge.","w":["This is 4.2 ÷ 7.0, measuring from the plate edge instead of the baseline.","","This is 3.2 ÷ 7.0, correcting the spot distance but not the solvent front distance.","This is 4.2 ÷ 6.0, correcting the solvent front distance but not the spot distance."]}
{"id":"ch-233","s":"chem","t":"3.3.16","lv":2,"q":"Mixture X gives two spots on a TLC plate, at 2.8 cm and 5.6 cm above the baseline; the solvent front is 7.0 cm above it. Same-solvent Rf values: P 0.40, Q 0.60, R 0.80. Which are in X?","o":["P and R","P and Q","Q and R","P, Q and R"],"a":0,"x":"Rf = distance moved ÷ solvent front distance. 2.8 ÷ 7.0 = 0.40 matches P and 5.6 ÷ 7.0 = 0.80 matches R. Q has no spot at 0.60, so it is absent. Two spots mean two components.","w":["","5.6 ÷ 7.0 is 0.80, not 0.60, so the second spot matches R rather than Q.","2.8 ÷ 7.0 is 0.40, not 0.60, so the first spot matches P rather than Q.","X shows only two spots, so it contains only two of the reference compounds."]}
{"id":"ch-234","s":"chem","t":"3.3.16","lv":1,"q":"In gas chromatography–mass spectrometry (GC–MS), what is the role of each part of the instrument?","o":["GC identifies each compound from its fragments, and MS separates them by retention time","GC separates the components of the mixture, and MS helps identify each one from its spectrum","GC and MS both separate the mixture, and neither can help to identify components","GC measures Rf values of the components, and MS records their boiling points"],"a":1,"x":"The GC column separates the mixture, giving different retention times. Each component then passes into the mass spectrometer, whose molecular ion and fragmentation pattern help identify it. The trap is swapping the two roles.","w":["This swaps the roles: GC separates by retention time and MS identifies from fragments.","","MS does not separate the mixture. Its spectra are used to identify the components.","Rf values belong to TLC, not GC, and MS gives m/z data rather than boiling points."]}
{"id":"ch-fig-001","s":"chem","t":"3.1.4","lv":2,"q":"Zinc powder is added to copper(II) sulfate solution at point M. The dashed line extrapolates the cooling curve back to the time of mixing. What temperature change should be used in q = mcΔT?","o":["10 K","12 K","32 K","6 K"],"a":1,"x":"Extrapolate the cooling line back to the time of mixing at M. It reaches 32 °C there, which allows for heat lost during the reaction. The start temperature is 20 °C, so ΔT = 32 − 20 = 12 K.","w":["Used the highest measured temperature, 30 °C, instead of the extrapolated value, so the heat lost while the reaction was happening is ignored.","","Read the extrapolated temperature but did not subtract the starting temperature, so this is a temperature, not a temperature change.","Subtracted the final reading, 26 °C, from the extrapolated 32 °C. This gives the cooling, not the rise in temperature caused by the reaction."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,10],"y":[18,34],"xstep":2,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"time / min","ylabel":"temperature / °C","lines":[{"pts":[[0,20],[4,20],[5,29],[6,30],[8,28],[10,26]],"c":0},{"pts":[[4,32],[10,26]],"dash":true,"c":1}],"points":[{"at":[4,20],"label":"M"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"ch-fig-002","s":"chem","t":"3.1.4","lv":3,"q":"In a thermometric titration, 25.0 cm³ of 2.00 mol dm⁻³ HCl is neutralised by 2.00 mol dm⁻³ NaOH. Both start at the same temperature. Use the graph to find ΔH of neutralisation. c = 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹, density 1.00 g cm⁻³.","o":["+58.5 kJ mol⁻¹","−29.3 kJ mol⁻¹","−58.5 kJ mol⁻¹","−117 kJ mol⁻¹"],"a":2,"x":"Peak at 25 cm³ NaOH, so total volume 50 cm³ (50 g). ΔT = 34 − 20 = 14 K. q = 50 × 4.18 × 14 = 2926 J. n(H₂O) = 0.0500 mol. ΔH = −2.926 ÷ 0.0500 = −58.5 kJ mol⁻¹.","w":["Correct size but the wrong sign. The temperature rises, so the reaction is exothermic and ΔH must be negative.","Used only 25 g of solution (the acid volume) instead of the total 50 g, which halves q and so halves ΔH.","","Used n = 0.0250 mol instead of 0.0500 mol, which doubles ΔH. 25.0 cm³ of 2.00 mol dm⁻³ HCl contains 0.0500 mol."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,40],"y":[20,36],"xstep":5,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"volume of NaOH added / cm³","ylabel":"temperature / °C","lines":[{"pts":[[0,20],[25,34],[40,30]],"c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"ch-fig-003","s":"chem","t":"3.1.5","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows the energy distribution of the same gas sample at two temperatures, A and B, with the activation energy marked. Which statement about the curves is correct?","o":["A is hotter: its higher peak means more molecules have E ≥ Ea","B is hotter: its area is larger, so there are more molecules in total","A is hotter: its peak is further left, so more molecules have E ≥ Ea","B is hotter: its peak is further right, so more molecules have E ≥ Ea"],"a":3,"x":"Raising the temperature moves the peak to higher energy and lowers and flattens it, as for curve B. The areas are equal because the number of molecules is fixed. B has far more area to the right of the Ea line.","w":["Takes the taller peak to mean hotter. Heating lowers and flattens the peak because the area stays the same. A has less area beyond Ea.","Thinks the areas differ. The same sample is used, so the areas are equal; B has more area beyond Ea, not more molecules.","Reads the peak position the wrong way round. A has its peak at lower energy and less area beyond Ea, so it is the cooler sample.",""],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,12],"y":[0,0.2],"xstep":2,"ystep":0.05,"xlabel":"energy","ylabel":"number of molecules","xnums":false,"ynums":false,"lines":[{"pts":[[0.0,0.0],[0.075,0.01806],[0.15,0.03479],[0.225,0.050265],[0.3,0.064553],[0.375,0.077721],[0.45,0.089833],[0.525,0.100948],[0.6,0.111123],[0.675,0.120412],[0.75,0.128867],[0.825,0.136536],[0.9,0.143466],[0.975,0.149701],[1.05,0.155283],[1.125,0.160251],[1.2,0.164643],[1.275,0.168495],[1.35,0.17184],[1.425,0.174711],[1.5,0.177137],[1.575,0.179149],[1.65,0.180772],[1.725,0.182033],[1.8,0.182956],[1.875,0.183565],[1.95,0.183881],[2.025,0.183925],[2.1,0.183717],[2.175,0.183275],[2.25,0.182617],[2.325,0.181759],[2.4,0.180717],[2.475,0.179505],[2.55,0.178137],[2.625,0.176627],[2.7,0.174987],[2.775,0.173229],[2.85,0.171362],[2.925,0.169399],[3.0,0.167348],[3.075,0.165218],[3.15,0.163018],[3.225,0.160